00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:01.099
“All at once the lands were removed and
scattered in the fray.

00:06:01.099 --> 00:06:04.349
No land could stand before their arms...

00:06:04.349 --> 00:06:12.560
They were coming forward toward Egypt, while
the flame was prepared before them."

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:16.909
Life proceeded in Egypt as it had for thousands
of years.

00:06:16.909 --> 00:06:21.920
High priests venerated monuments to the pharaohs
of old, while preaching cryptic wisdom of

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:23.310
the gods.

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:28.210
Camels carried travelers across searing dunes,
and crocodiles cooled their scales in the

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:29.880
flowing Nile.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:35.110
Along its banks, common folk tended to the
fertile earth by day, and spent cold nights

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:38.900
cloistered in their mud-brick dwellings.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:46.650
And above it all, on an ornate throne amidst
monuments to his own glory, sat a king beset

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:48.340
by tortured thoughts.

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:54.940
The 42-year-old Pharaoh Ramesses III was the
living incarnation of the avian god Horus,

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:58.300
said to will the very movement of the sun
and the moon.

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:03.889
But Ramesses had been fated to rule over a
bloody age.

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:14.069
It was 1175 BC, and the very idea of civilization
itself was in danger.

00:07:14.069 --> 00:07:21.919
An existential threat stalked the Mediterranean:
powerful confederations of seafaring tribes

00:07:21.919 --> 00:07:28.270
whose ranks were filled with masterful sailors,
savvy traders, and tenacious pirates.

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:31.270
Coastal cities were engulfed in flames.

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:36.729
“They desolated its people, and its land
was like that which has never come into being.”

00:07:36.729 --> 00:07:40.830
[e]
Ramesses watched as the pirates ushered in

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:45.000
the Bronze Age collapse, seeing his rivals
fall to ruin.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:48.900
Syria, the Levant, Cyprus, and Greece would
crumble.

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:52.349
The final hour of Egypt must have been close.

00:07:52.349 --> 00:07:57.249
As these proto-pirates charted course to the
Nile, the Pharaoh called forth men from all

00:07:57.249 --> 00:08:01.449
corners of the kingdom and furnished them
with shining lances, bows, and chariots.

00:08:01.449 --> 00:08:03.101
“They were like lions roaring upon the mountain
tops.”[f]

00:08:03.101 --> 00:08:04.660
The enemy flooded into the delta.

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:09.650
Their light vessels carried raiders clad in
all manner of armor, but undivided in their

00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:13.379
determination to topple the old world.

00:08:13.379 --> 00:08:15.590
A hail of arrows rained down.

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:20.340
The Egyptians charged with lances, squeezed
their opponents at a choke point, and ran

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:21.689
their ships aground.

00:08:21.689 --> 00:08:25.930
Then, they ripped the pirates onto the sand
and hacked them to death.

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:29.259
[g]
“They were dragged in, enclosed, and prostrated

00:08:29.259 --> 00:08:33.440
on the beach, killed, and made into heaps
from tail to head.”

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:39.550
Finally, a grisly red tide beat against the
sides of overturned ships.

00:08:39.550 --> 00:08:49.300
Ramesses had prevailed, and the onslaught
of the piratical Sea Peoples was halted..

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:54.110
And so, it was this apocalyptic Battle of
the Delta that was memorialized on the outer

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:55.839
walls of Medinet Habu.

00:08:55.839 --> 00:09:04.220
It may be the earliest depiction of a pirate
attack in existence.

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:10.620
It is likely that for as long as people have
elected to transport things of value over

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:15.139
the ocean, [h]there have existed people who
endeavored to steal those things.

00:09:15.139 --> 00:09:20.921
But if piracy comprises robbery and violence
on the high seas and coastal areas, the Sea

00:09:20.921 --> 00:09:26.400
Peoples showed that its practitioners need
not be mere ship-borne bandits.

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:31.769
On the contrary, the Sea Peoples traded, migrated,
and prudently navigated political allegiances.

00:09:31.769 --> 00:09:37.190
Indeed, the pre-Classical Ancient Greeks understood
that piracy had once been considered an entirely

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:39.940
legitimate source of financial sustenance.

00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:44.280
The jagged edges of the Mediterranean were
unsuitable for large-scale agriculture, but

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:47.160
passing trade ships hugged the coastlines.

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:52.300
Like tempting sirens, they beckoned sailors
and fishermen toward a treacherous life of

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:53.300
piracy.

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:58.680
“[Barbarians] were tempted to turn pirates,
under the conduct of their most powerful men;

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.740
the motives being to serve their own greed
and to support the needy.

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:06.100
They would fall upon a town unprotected by
walls, and consisting of a mere collection

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:12.260
of villages, and would plunder it; indeed,
this came to be the main source of their livelihood,

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:18.100
no disgrace being yet attached to such an
achievement, but even some glory.”[i]

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:22.370
While small bands of piratical opportunists
proved an inevitable reality of the ancient

00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:28.810
world, many of the most fervent practitioners
of piracy were more legitimate states or confederacies.

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:33.810
Entire peoples like the Illyrians and Phoenecians
participated in large scale trading and cultural

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:37.960
exchange, in addition to fairly indiscriminate
piracy.

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:41.560
But pirates nevertheless thrived on instability.

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:45.750
In a pattern that would characterize piracy
for millennia to come, places that had fallen

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:51.230
into ruin due to war or neglect became the
most attractive pirate dens— the cracked

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:56.350
vestiges of the Minoan civilization on Crete
and islands scarred by Peloponnesian conflict

00:10:56.350 --> 00:11:00.420
became bases for pirates to organize raids
on ships and coasts, taking goods and slaves

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:01.420
with them.

00:11:01.420 --> 00:11:02.420
But the winds of empire threatened piracy.

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:03.420
As Alexander toppled cities and the star of
Rome enveloped Greece, pirate hegemony waned.

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:04.420
Rome used accusations of piracy as a justification
for conquest.

00:11:04.420 --> 00:11:05.420
Naturally, such accusations were fraught with
inconsistency and hypocrisy.

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:06.420
[j]Ancient pirates conquered ill-guarded land,
pillaged ships and coastlines, and conducted

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:10.500
slave raids in a manner not dissimilar from
contemporary empires.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:15.019
Defining which kinds of ship-borne robbery
fell outside of the law would continue to

00:11:15.019 --> 00:11:34.139
be a matter of perspective 
and convenience.

00:11:34.139 --> 00:11:38.490
The Northern Greek Aetolian League, which
had practiced large-scale piracy against its

00:11:38.490 --> 00:11:43.199
enemies, was defeated by the Roman Republic
in 192 BC.

00:11:43.199 --> 00:11:47.640
But the Romans had simply pushed the issue
beyond their gaze.

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:51.450
The Aetolian pirates fled and set up operations
in the nook of Asia Minor, where they garnered

00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:52.450
the support of local kings too weak to drive
them out.

00:11:52.450 --> 00:11:56.260
Based in the dilapidated towns of Cilicia,
the most fearsome pirate community the ancient

00:11:56.260 --> 00:12:00.149
world had ever seen now swarmed the Mediterranean.

00:12:00.149 --> 00:12:07.931
In their voracious slave-raiding, these Cilician
pirates made friends in powerful places.

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:17.130
The hostage refused to stay quiet.

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:20.130
In truth, his captors found him amusing.

00:12:20.130 --> 00:12:24.370
Drunken cackling of sunburnt pirates echoed
along the coast as they watched him perform

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:30.120
poems and speeches with boyish pluck, the
warm, turquoise borders of the Eastern Mediterranean

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:34.050
his tranquil prison.[l]
The confused Cilicians listened intently to

00:12:34.050 --> 00:12:35.690
the brash young Roman.

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:39.769
Their hostage had suggested they raise the
ransom for his freedom to more accurately

00:12:39.769 --> 00:12:44.880
reflect his status, and the Cilicians had
complied.

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:49.339
His countrymen may have controlled the Western
Mediterranean, but the eastern half remained

00:12:49.339 --> 00:12:50.850
pirate territory.

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:55.060
The Cilicians attacked with little regard
for the allegiances of their victims.

00:12:55.060 --> 00:13:00.490
The powerful landowners and senators of Rome
nevertheless tolerated them, as the profitability

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:06.250
of their sweeping plantations depended on
a stable relationship with the pirates.

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:10.300
For peacetime in the Republic made for bad
business.

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:15.459
Without conquered peoples to be sold in Roman
markets, Roman landowners turned to the Cilician

00:13:15.459 --> 00:13:19.019
pirates, who eagerly sold them people they
had captured in slave raids.

00:13:19.019 --> 00:13:20.019
While Rome stood as a shining bastion of civilization,
the pirate underworld was her concealed accomplice.

00:13:20.019 --> 00:13:25.019
If pirates were to roam the seas, empires
would continue to find ways to use their presence

00:13:25.019 --> 00:13:26.199
to their advantage.

00:13:26.199 --> 00:13:32.829
But Cilician looting of Roman shipping also
created fervent enemies in Rome, and Rome

00:13:32.829 --> 00:13:35.399
began to enact antipiracy laws.

00:13:35.399 --> 00:13:39.610
The derision with which the Roman hostage
treated his captors may have reflected this

00:13:39.610 --> 00:13:41.180
change in attitudes.

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:45.260
When he wished to sleep, the Roman would interrupt
the late-night revelry of the pirates and

00:13:45.260 --> 00:13:46.990
tell them to stop talking.

00:13:46.990 --> 00:13:52.020
In passing, he muttered teasing threats to
have his captors executed.

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:58.315
After a month, the ransom came, and the young
captive, named Julius Caesar, sailed off.

00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:05.519
The pirates decided to linger on the coast
a little while longer, bobbing at anchor and

00:14:05.519 --> 00:14:07.730
basking in their earnings.

00:14:07.730 --> 00:14:11.780
But their celebrations were soon cut short.

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:14.610
A fleet appeared on the horizon.

00:14:14.610 --> 00:14:17.160
Caesar had returned.

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:20.829
He had the pirates thrown in prison and reclaimed
the ransom money.

00:14:20.829 --> 00:14:26.041
“He went to Pergamon, took the pirates out
of prison and crucified the lot of them, just

00:14:26.041 --> 00:14:31.220
as he had often told them he would do when
he was on the island and they imagined that

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:32.220
he was joking.”

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:41.730
But Cilician pirates continued to undermine
the stability of the Roman Republic.

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:47.779
Indeed when Spartacus launched a slave revolt
two years later, in 73 BC, the Cilicians supported

00:14:47.779 --> 00:14:48.779
his efforts.

00:14:48.779 --> 00:14:53.040
Though when Spartacus met with the pirates
and paid them to transport 2,000 of his rebels

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:57.970
to Sicily, the Cilicians took the gifts and
sailed off without them.

00:14:57.970 --> 00:15:02.620
They then burned the Roman port of Ostia and
kidnapped two Roman senators.

00:15:02.620 --> 00:15:07.980
Now, common Romans felt the material shock
on grain supplies and the economy.

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:13.560
They looked to the rising General Pompey to
confront the issue, and he gathered support

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:19.160
for a costly mission: the complete annihilation
of the Cilician pirates.

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:22.290
The Senate passed the “Gabinian Law.”

00:15:22.290 --> 00:15:27.139
“The law gave him dominion over the sea
this side of the pillars of Hercules, over

00:15:27.139 --> 00:15:31.399
all the mainland to the distance of four hundred
furlongs from the sea.

00:15:31.399 --> 00:15:35.680
These limits included almost all places in
the Roman world, and the greatest nations

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:38.120
and most powerful kings were comprised within
them.

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:39.120
Besides this, he was empowered to... take
from the public treasuries and the tax-collectors

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:40.120
as much money as he wished, and to have two
hundred ships, with full power over the number

00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:41.120
and levying of soldiers and oarsmen.”

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:44.079
With over a hundred thousand troops at his
disposal, Pompey sent forth his army to hound

00:15:44.079 --> 00:15:45.720
the pirates at every corner.

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:50.690
They blockaded and demolished pirate lairs
and drove many of the stragglers back to Cilicia.

00:15:50.690 --> 00:15:55.540
Roman ships followed them and formed impenetrable
rows of blockades, forcing the pirates onto

00:15:55.540 --> 00:15:56.620
land.

00:15:56.620 --> 00:16:01.570
From there, the Roman troops slaughtered the
pirates with mechanical efficiency.

00:16:01.570 --> 00:16:04.040
Upon their surrender, Pompey had Cilician
leaders killed.

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:07.480
The others, he showed mercy.

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:13.759
“Reflecting, therefore, that by nature man
neither is nor becomes a wild or an unsocial

00:16:13.759 --> 00:16:18.759
creature, but is transformed by the unnatural
practice of vice...he determined to transfer

00:16:18.759 --> 00:16:24.480
the men from the sea to land, and let them
have a taste of gentle life by being accustomed

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:32.029
to dwell in cities and to till the ground”
Pompey had tamed the seas.

00:16:32.029 --> 00:16:40.449
In all, he had razed 120 pirate bases, destroyed
500 ships, and killed 10,000 pirates.

00:16:40.449 --> 00:16:46.529
A Pax Romana washed over the Mediterranean;
and in the coming centuries, friends of Rome

00:16:46.529 --> 00:16:49.589
were free to sail without serious danger.

00:16:49.589 --> 00:16:54.380
Piracy, it seemed, had been defeated once
and for all.

00:16:54.380 --> 00:17:01.988
So long as the Eternal City of Rome stood,
the western pirate would remain extinct.

00:17:11.959 --> 00:17:15.900
A shout echoed through the cold corridors
of a castle.

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:20.850
Pirate brethren scrambled to their posts,
and peered beyond the walls.

00:17:20.850 --> 00:17:26.900
Cutting across soft mounds of snow, was a
line of clattering iron and black crosses.

00:17:26.900 --> 00:17:32.120
The pirates readied their feeble defenses,
as the Teutonic Knights marched with gleaming

00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:34.690
swords and crossbows.

00:17:34.690 --> 00:17:40.590
The soldiers of Christ had come to carry out
a royal bounty, a quest to slaughter the doomed

00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:47.240
pirates in their fortress and finally end
medieval piracy - a bloody era of seaborne

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:57.010
banditry inaugurated centuries earlier, by
heathens.

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:07.240
“...[T]he pagans from the northern regions
came with a naval force to Britain like stinging

00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:14.460
hornets and spread on all sides like fearful
wolves, robbed, tore and slaughtered not only

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:20.130
beasts of burden, sheep and oxen, but even
priests and deacons, and companies of monks

00:18:20.130 --> 00:18:22.350
and nuns.

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:27.510
And they came to the church of Lindisfarne,
laid everything waste with grievous plundering,

00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:32.920
trampled the holy places with polluted steps,
dug up the altars and seized all the treasures

00:18:32.920 --> 00:18:34.320
of the holy church.

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:39.500
They killed some of the brothers, took some
away with them in fetters, many they drove

00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:46.370
out, naked and loaded with insults, some they
drowned in the sea..."

00:18:46.370 --> 00:18:51.580
Through beards doused in frothy mead, men
of the north orchestrated the downfall of

00:18:51.580 --> 00:18:53.309
far-off kingdoms.

00:18:53.309 --> 00:18:57.510
In their mead halls, they gathered before
a crackling hearth and wooden throne in a

00:18:57.510 --> 00:19:03.080
quest for untold riches and immortal glory
in Valhalla.

00:19:03.080 --> 00:19:07.460
The fractured nations of northern Europe had
left shining treasures and exotic goods on

00:19:07.460 --> 00:19:13.510
their shores, exposed and ready to be plundered.

00:19:13.510 --> 00:19:18.480
Viking piracy did not constitute robbery on
the high seas, but instead took the form of

00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:21.940
lightning coastal raids.

00:19:21.940 --> 00:19:26.690
Ship-borne attacks on wherever they saw wealth
earned them precious artefacts and innumerable

00:19:26.690 --> 00:19:27.690
slaves.

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:33.130
Over the centuries, disjointed raids on small
villages and monasteries grew to fully fledged

00:19:33.130 --> 00:19:38.630
excursions upon cities and vast territories.

00:19:38.630 --> 00:19:44.830
These Vikings, or raiders, terrorized the
distant corners of Europe - the audacious

00:19:44.830 --> 00:19:50.650
Hasting and Bjorn Ironside ventured as far
as Islamic Spain, sowing chaos on the sunkissed

00:19:50.650 --> 00:19:52.740
Guadallquvir river in Seville.

00:19:52.740 --> 00:19:53.740
They went on to burn and pillage Southern
France and Italy, terrorizing Mediterranean

00:19:53.740 --> 00:19:54.740
waters from which the watchful eye of Rome
had retreated long ago.

00:19:54.740 --> 00:19:57.700
For the stability of the Western Roman Empire
had long since collapsed.

00:19:57.700 --> 00:20:01.310
Though the Byzantine Empire took up the Roman
mantle in the east, they held no real sway

00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:02.770
in Rome´s heartland.

00:20:02.770 --> 00:20:09.530
And soon, Muslim factions had gained footholds
in North Africa, Iberia, and the Middle East.

00:20:09.530 --> 00:20:15.370
Then came the Crusades, and the Byzantine
Empire too fractured into a series of rump

00:20:15.370 --> 00:20:18.059
states, ceasing to exist.

00:20:18.059 --> 00:20:21.710
In the ruins of this second empire, pirates
returned.

00:20:21.710 --> 00:20:26.900
The very sailors who had once filled the ranks
of the Byzantine navy set up hideouts at its

00:20:26.900 --> 00:20:31.880
crumbling corners, and began to ambush merchant
ships.

00:20:31.880 --> 00:20:36.750
Rumblings from the East then foretold a new
wave of bloodshed— an unstoppable horde

00:20:36.750 --> 00:20:42.059
of steppe warriors rode west under the banner
of Genghis Khan.

00:20:42.059 --> 00:20:48.070
With pirates infesting the coasts and Mongols
at the gates, a claimant to the defunct Byzantine

00:20:48.070 --> 00:20:54.340
throne emerged: Michael VIII retook Constantinople
and resurrected an empire which had laid dormant

00:20:54.340 --> 00:20:56.000
for six decades.

00:20:56.000 --> 00:21:01.240
But the return of Byzantine authority did
not guarantee stability.

00:21:01.240 --> 00:21:06.029
Unable to muster a true national navy, he
decided to weaponize Mediterranean pirates

00:21:06.029 --> 00:21:07.620
for Byzantine ends.

00:21:07.620 --> 00:21:14.990
He called pirate leaders from their remote
hideouts, and turned them into his privateers—

00:21:14.990 --> 00:21:20.090
sailors who could use their own ships to plunder
under Byzantine authority.

00:21:20.090 --> 00:21:25.090
Legitimate piracy was born.[m]
This would set a precedent for the centuries

00:21:25.090 --> 00:21:26.500
to come.

00:21:26.500 --> 00:21:32.600
Pirates were above all shapeshifters— opportunists
disposed to true piracy at times, and the

00:21:32.600 --> 00:21:35.960
legal protection of privateering at others.

00:21:35.960 --> 00:21:40.730
Men who had for years ruled as pirates, now
justified their pillaging and violence against

00:21:40.730 --> 00:21:45.640
Italian trade under the banner of the Byzantine
Empire.

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:50.000
For a cash-strapped kingdom, seaborne mercenaries
presented a viable alternative to a legitimate

00:21:50.390 --> 00:21:56.798
navy, and Northern Europe would soon realize
this.

00:22:01.429 --> 00:22:08.740
The deposed Swedish King Albert sat in a dungeon,
in desperate need of aid.

00:22:08.740 --> 00:22:14.470
Scattered beyond his city of Stockholm were
forested specks of land, sat in dark and briny

00:22:14.470 --> 00:22:15.470
waters.

00:22:15.470 --> 00:22:19.809
Glacial currents shimmered with herring, and
bent under the strain of hostile ships that

00:22:19.809 --> 00:22:21.480
infested the harbor.

00:22:21.480 --> 00:22:28.130
This was a suffocating blockade: a gift from
the new Swedish monarch, Margaret of Denmark.

00:22:28.130 --> 00:22:33.120
At roughly forty years of age, the shrewd
Queen Margaret claimed rulership over all

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:35.190
of Scandinavia.

00:22:35.190 --> 00:22:40.840
The dark-haired queen, known to be beautiful
and intimidating in equal measure, was hellbent

00:22:40.840 --> 00:22:44.200
on uniting the region at all costs.

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:52.340
Thus, as Stockholm remained loyal to King
Albert, Margaret decided to starve the city.

00:22:52.340 --> 00:22:55.350
No ordinary sailor could penetrate the swarm.

00:22:55.350 --> 00:23:00.070
The allies of King Albert turned instead to
professionals.

00:23:00.070 --> 00:23:04.190
Handsomely-paid German mercenaries were sent
to slip through the blockade and run supplies

00:23:04.190 --> 00:23:05.190
into Stockholm.

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:07.450
These armed men soon acted as privateers,
wreaking havoc on the merchant vessels of

00:23:07.450 --> 00:23:08.870
Margaret and her allies.

00:23:08.870 --> 00:23:14.510
Together, they formed a guild called the Victual
Brothers.

00:23:14.510 --> 00:23:19.160
In need of a safe base of operations, the
Victual Brothers took the town Visby on the

00:23:19.160 --> 00:23:20.000
island of Gotland, one of Margaret’s Swedish
territories.

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:29.541
And In 1394, the unabashed pirates became
the de facto rulers of Gotland.

00:23:34.392 --> 00:23:37.630
The seas were chaos.

00:23:37.630 --> 00:23:42.140
The Victual Brothers assailed so many passing
ships that trade became impossible.

00:23:42.140 --> 00:23:43.960
Economies floundered.

00:23:43.960 --> 00:23:47.289
The fishing industry was halted and food supplies
dwindled.

00:23:47.289 --> 00:23:50.419
In exasperated meetings, Margaret called for
aid.

00:23:50.419 --> 00:23:55.299
Finally, it was an old order of knights that
took up the Queen’s quest.

00:23:55.299 --> 00:24:00.820
Based in Prussia, the Crusaders of the Teutonic
Order came forward, with troops prepared with

00:24:00.820 --> 00:24:03.570
broad swords and exquisite shining armor.

00:24:03.570 --> 00:24:09.070
They boasted menacing visored helmets and
shields bearing black crosses.

00:24:09.070 --> 00:24:13.760
The knights desired to control Gotland; Queen
Margaret promised to grant their wish if they

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:16.360
could clear the island of the pirates.

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:24.460
And in the winter of 1397-1398, the Victual
Brothers docked their ships.

00:24:24.460 --> 00:24:29.450
Almost all came ashore to weather the Scandinavian
cold on Gotland.

00:24:29.450 --> 00:24:33.230
The Knights and their Prussian troops sailed
out in secret to meet them.

00:24:33.230 --> 00:24:40.990
84 ships and 4,000 men were to attack under
the leadership of Grandmaster Konrad von Jungingen.

00:24:40.990 --> 00:24:44.020
They clambered ashore and met the pirates
in the snow.

00:24:44.020 --> 00:24:48.100
As negotiations for peace took place, the
Knights and their Prussian army marched to

00:24:48.100 --> 00:24:49.960
every corner of the island.

00:24:49.960 --> 00:24:55.340
They found the pirates tucked away in castles
and forts; they laid siege to their defenses,

00:24:55.340 --> 00:24:59.470
and hacked to death and filled with arrows
the Victual Brothers they found.

00:24:59.470 --> 00:25:03.960
The knights finally demolished the city of
Visby and conquered the island.

00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:07.309
They afforded pirates three days to leave
the island.

00:25:07.309 --> 00:25:11.100
Those who lingered were captured and killed.

00:25:11.100 --> 00:25:16.360
Leaders like Klaus Störtebeker continued
pirating for years, but even he was caught,

00:25:16.360 --> 00:25:19.970
brought in, and executed with his crew.

00:25:19.970 --> 00:25:25.490
If European waters had truly fallen into the
hands of powerful kings and queens like Margaret,

00:25:25.490 --> 00:25:29.890
then piracy would need to move beyond their
gaze.

00:25:29.890 --> 00:25:34.040
Perhaps there was money to be made further
afield.

00:25:34.040 --> 00:25:40.320
And indeed, in a century’s time, Europeans
would make dangerous forays into an uncharted

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:41.669
ocean.

00:25:41.669 --> 00:25:48.270
Explorers and conquistadors stumbled upon
a new hemisphere of jungles, tropical islands,

00:25:48.270 --> 00:25:53.100
and vast civilizations - brimming with wealth.

00:25:53.100 --> 00:25:59.658
Pirates, and politically useful privateers,
would soon take notice.

00:26:05.674 --> 00:26:10.730
Eyes of a scowling Aztec god set into priceless
gold.

00:26:10.730 --> 00:26:16.580
Chests of clattering gems, lumps of silver,
and sacred headdresses.

00:26:16.580 --> 00:26:22.429
A live jaguar baring its teeth with every
sway of the ship.

00:26:22.429 --> 00:26:28.880
And a Spanish sailor trying his best not to
make eye contact.

00:26:28.880 --> 00:26:32.670
Stolen souvenirs from the New World— courtesy
of Hernan Cortes.

00:26:32.670 --> 00:26:38.860
Cortes and his ironclad conquistadors had
waded through the Mexican jungle, orchestrating

00:26:38.860 --> 00:26:42.700
a large indigenous resistance against Aztec
rule.

00:26:42.700 --> 00:26:47.640
They had then used the chaos to wrest control
of the region and bleed the crumbling empire

00:26:47.640 --> 00:26:50.919
of Montezuma of its abundant wealth.

00:26:50.919 --> 00:26:56.610
Now, the trio of Spanish ships creaked home,
heavy with treasure.

00:26:56.610 --> 00:27:00.720
The sailors were to guard the secret cargo
at all costs.

00:27:00.720 --> 00:27:05.040
Their gaze swept over the horizon as they
awaited a pair of armed vessels to escort

00:27:05.040 --> 00:27:08.200
them on the last leg of their journey.

00:27:08.200 --> 00:27:14.820
But instead, one by one, strange shapes rose
above the curve of the Atlantic.

00:27:14.820 --> 00:27:20.110
Five mysterious boats swarmed after the Spanish
trio.

00:27:20.110 --> 00:27:24.539
The treasure-laden ships groaned and clanked
as the Spaniards wheeled hard in the opposite

00:27:24.539 --> 00:27:25.539
direction.

00:27:25.539 --> 00:27:30.330
The attackers dogged them for hours, until
at last, they apprehended two of them.

00:27:30.330 --> 00:27:36.080
With sharpened blades and feathered flamboyance,
Captain Jean Fleury and his French crew demanded

00:27:36.080 --> 00:27:42.120
they surrender, lest they come aboard by force,
and duly butcher every Spaniard they saw.

00:27:42.120 --> 00:27:46.520
The exhausted Spaniards surrendered without
fanfare, and the Frenchmen came aboard to

00:27:46.520 --> 00:27:51.300
haul away the treasure, chest by chest.

00:27:54.419 --> 00:27:59.690
In the eyes of France, Jean Fleury was not
a criminal, but a hero - for he possessed

00:27:59.690 --> 00:28:03.630
a document called a letter of marque.

00:28:03.630 --> 00:28:07.940
These were licenses issued during war, which
offered sailors the legal permission of their

00:28:07.940 --> 00:28:12.470
home country to hunt, attack, and capture
non-combat vessels of rival nations.

00:28:12.470 --> 00:28:16.960
Men whoß might have otherwise been hunted
as pirates, could function as private agents

00:28:16.960 --> 00:28:24.340
of the state, or privateers, allowed to declare
and keep most of the loot they plundered.

00:28:24.340 --> 00:28:28.600
Privateers like Fleury were the product of
a changing Europe, whose fractured kingdoms

00:28:28.600 --> 00:28:31.500
had begun to crystallize into potent empires.

00:28:31.500 --> 00:28:36.210
The Kingdom of France had risen in competition
with the other established monarchies of Spain,

00:28:36.210 --> 00:28:38.890
Portugal, and the Holy Roman Empire.

00:28:38.890 --> 00:28:43.390
Blocked in the east by the Muslim Ottoman
Empire, these warring Western European dynasties

00:28:43.390 --> 00:28:48.150
began seeking alternative routes to the lucrative
East Indies.

00:28:48.150 --> 00:28:54.150
Columbus had made landfall on American soil
in 1492, and now conquistadors sailed about

00:28:54.150 --> 00:28:58.440
the Americas in search of glory, land, and
treasure.

00:28:58.440 --> 00:29:04.080
But if Spain had truly landed in a new world,
her burgeoning empire would become the natural

00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:07.059
target of her rivals.

00:29:07.059 --> 00:29:12.130
Even after his capture, as Jean Fleury swung
from the gallows, the Frenchman had set something

00:29:12.130 --> 00:29:13.370
in motion.

00:29:13.370 --> 00:29:17.640
His privateering had revealed to the world
what riches the Spanish had uncovered in their

00:29:17.640 --> 00:29:23.460
grisly New World conquests, and placed a target
on their treasure-laden vessels.

00:29:23.460 --> 00:29:27.950
In 1536, the French attacked Havana and other
Spanish New World ports.

00:29:27.950 --> 00:29:34.470
The following year, French privateers cut
in half the Spanish income from the New World.

00:29:34.470 --> 00:29:38.890
Spain recognized its treasure ships were in
the most dire need of protection, so the Crown

00:29:38.890 --> 00:29:41.049
developed the flota system.

00:29:41.049 --> 00:29:44.570
No longer would treasure ships travel exposed
across the Atlantic.

00:29:44.570 --> 00:29:47.840
Well-armed convoys were rallied to escort
them.

00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:52.350
Seaside forts with armored soldiers and cannons
would also be raised around key ports.

00:29:52.350 --> 00:29:57.440
And in the wake of peace between Spain and
France in 1559 French letters of marque dried

00:29:57.440 --> 00:30:01.899
up and legitimate French privateers disappeared
from the Spanish Main.

00:30:01.899 --> 00:30:06.019
But that was far from the end.

00:30:06.019 --> 00:30:12.110
For across the channel, Elizabeth I took up
their mantle - The English Queen, a staunch

00:30:12.110 --> 00:30:16.720
Protestant, viewed Spain as a bitter political
and religious rival.

00:30:16.720 --> 00:30:24.898
Armed with her own letters of marque, she
let loose her Sea Dogs.

00:30:31.360 --> 00:30:36.169
Camped at the edge of a chirping jungle stood
a goateed adventurer - an intrepid Englishman

00:30:36.169 --> 00:30:38.160
with vengeance in his heart.

00:30:38.160 --> 00:30:43.809
For this secret mission, Francis Drake had
assembled a band of loot-hungry individuals—

00:30:43.809 --> 00:30:49.399
English privateers like himself, the French
buccaneer Le Tetsu, and a network of savvy

00:30:49.399 --> 00:30:51.559
runaway slaves.

00:30:51.559 --> 00:30:57.179
Drake had tracked the Spanish treasure shipments
all the way here, to the Isthmus of Panama.

00:30:57.179 --> 00:31:01.860
In the morning, mule loads would secretly
cart South American treasures to the city

00:31:01.860 --> 00:31:06.720
of Nombre de Dios, before they were shipped
back to Spain.

00:31:06.720 --> 00:31:12.809
The son of a farmer, Drake desired most of
all riches and glory - The Spanish had thwarted

00:31:12.809 --> 00:31:17.540
his commercial endeavors in the New World
- they were a natural target.

00:31:17.540 --> 00:31:19.950
Bells rang out.

00:31:19.950 --> 00:31:21.760
The caravan approached.

00:31:21.760 --> 00:31:27.740
200 mules labored forward, laden with 30 tons
of New World treasure.

00:31:27.740 --> 00:31:30.820
Dozens of Spanish soldiers marched alongside
them.

00:31:30.820 --> 00:31:32.640
Drake met them head on.

00:31:32.640 --> 00:31:34.820
He recounted,
“...in the end, these soldiers thought it

00:31:34.820 --> 00:31:39.299
the best way to leave their mules with us,
and to seek for more help abroad...In which

00:31:39.299 --> 00:31:44.580
meantime we took some pain to ease some of
the mules which were heaviest loaden of their

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:45.580
carriage.

00:31:45.580 --> 00:31:49.400
And because we ourselves were somewhat weary,
we were contented with a few bars and quoits

00:31:49.400 --> 00:31:54.710
of gold, as we could well carry: burying about
fifteen tons of silver…”

00:31:54.710 --> 00:31:56.870
Spanish reinforcements would arrive any minute.

00:31:56.870 --> 00:32:02.019
With glowing gold in their arms, Dake and
his men marched for miles under damp vines

00:32:02.019 --> 00:32:03.320
and dangling vipers.

00:32:03.320 --> 00:32:06.529
Drake and his crew neared the end of the mountainous
jungle.

00:32:06.529 --> 00:32:10.440
At last, they stumbled onto the beach where
they had left their ships, only to find that

00:32:10.440 --> 00:32:11.909
they had disappeared.

00:32:11.909 --> 00:32:15.360
Spanish vessels loomed along the coastline
in their place.

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:20.020
The pirates could see their capture and death
were imminent.

00:32:20.020 --> 00:32:24.860
Drake acted quickly; he ordered the men to
bury their gold and make a raft from the trees.

00:32:24.860 --> 00:32:30.159
They drifted ten miles down the coast until
they reached his flagship, and safety.

00:32:30.159 --> 00:32:34.929
Drake later returned to recover the gold,
took a captain’s portion for himself, and

00:32:34.929 --> 00:32:38.309
divided the rest amongst the French and English.

00:32:41.763 --> 00:32:46.140
Francis Drake’s first foray into the world
of privateering would enable an illustrious

00:32:46.140 --> 00:32:47.140
career.

00:32:47.140 --> 00:32:52.039
He returned to England a celebrity of sorts,
and forged a close relationship with the Queen,

00:32:52.039 --> 00:32:56.980
who affectionately referred to him as “my
pirate.”, sending him on various unofficial

00:32:56.980 --> 00:32:59.899
missions to plunder the ships of her Spanish
rivals.

00:32:59.899 --> 00:33:06.289
In his elegant vessel, the Golden Hind, Drake
circumnavigated the planet, harassing and

00:33:06.289 --> 00:33:09.380
plundering Spanish ports and ships along the
way.

00:33:09.380 --> 00:33:13.970
Driven more by opportunity than any lofty
ideals, Drake could just as easily plunder

00:33:13.970 --> 00:33:18.269
the Spanish Main shoulder-to-shoulder with
runaway slaves, as he could sell captured

00:33:18.269 --> 00:33:20.470
Africans into slavery.

00:33:20.470 --> 00:33:25.340
And after warding off the Spanish Armada’s
attack on England, Drake became something

00:33:25.340 --> 00:33:27.000
of a national hero.

00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:30.409
Of course, self-interest could underlie his
actions at any time.

00:33:30.409 --> 00:33:35.830
In 1588, when tasked with shadowing the invading
Spanish armada, he defied orders and instead

00:33:35.830 --> 00:33:38.049
hunted after a treasure-filled ship.

00:33:38.049 --> 00:33:42.929
Since the Crown took its cut of all privateer
plunder, Drake hid half of this stolen gold

00:33:42.929 --> 00:33:46.480
before returning to London, and then bought
himself a mansion.

00:33:46.480 --> 00:33:51.360
This was the unique duality of privateering,
where greed and self-interest could shakily

00:33:51.360 --> 00:33:53.880
coexist with national service.

00:33:53.880 --> 00:33:59.250
French, English, and Dutch privateers readily
hoisted their sails for the promise of untold

00:33:59.250 --> 00:34:02.779
wealth, and were hailed as rugged heroes in
their home countries.

00:34:02.779 --> 00:34:08.419
An account of Drake’s exploits, cosigned
by the man himself, was titled: Sir Francis

00:34:08.419 --> 00:34:15.690
Drake Revived: Calling Upon This Dull or Effeminate
Age, to Follow His Noble Steps for Gold and

00:34:15.690 --> 00:34:18.830
Silver.

00:34:18.830 --> 00:34:23.460
Though peace between Spain and England would
come, and the era of unchecked privateering

00:34:23.460 --> 00:34:30.470
would pass, that same century saw the terrifying
rise of a pirate power farther east.

00:34:30.470 --> 00:34:35.730
Perched on the northern edge of Africa, Muslim
pirates attacked the very heart of the Christian

00:34:35.730 --> 00:34:38.980
empires that laid claim to the Americas.

00:34:38.980 --> 00:34:45.090
Conquistadors left American soil and returned
to the Mediterranean in anxious haste.

00:34:45.090 --> 00:34:49.295
A storm was brewing.

00:34:55.980 --> 00:35:01.290
It was said that until the late nineteenth
century, a mountain of skulls stood on the

00:35:01.290 --> 00:35:03.870
Tunisian island of Djerba.

00:35:03.870 --> 00:35:10.880
Passersby gazed upon a yellowed tower of human
bone— sunken faces with mouths agape.

00:35:10.880 --> 00:35:15.590
The heads had been harvested from Spanish
sailors three centuries prior.

00:35:15.590 --> 00:35:25.790
They were the victims of a man called Dragut.

00:35:25.790 --> 00:35:28.660
The newest slave brought with him notoriety.

00:35:28.660 --> 00:35:33.541
Rumors about him spread throughout the stinking
Italian ship: tales of his escapades, when

00:35:33.541 --> 00:35:38.720
black stacks of smoke had bloomed along the
Italian coast and his pointed ships carried

00:35:38.720 --> 00:35:40.600
away goods and people.

00:35:40.600 --> 00:35:46.820
They said he was a great Barbary pirate, by
the name of Dragut.

00:35:46.820 --> 00:35:52.080
Whispers spread that he had even trained under
the famed corsair Hayreddin Barbarossa—

00:35:52.080 --> 00:35:56.530
together felling Italian fortresses and capturing
precious merchant ships from distant infidels.

00:35:56.530 --> 00:36:00.180
The rumours were true.

00:36:00.180 --> 00:36:05.650
Like all Barbary corsairs, Dragut had acted
as a pirate or privateer, ravaging Christian

00:36:05.650 --> 00:36:10.530
coastlines and exchanging a cut of the loot
for safety and status in the Islamic coastal

00:36:10.530 --> 00:36:15.290
polities of North Africa, or, the so-called
Barbary States.

00:36:15.290 --> 00:36:20.080
The region offered little in the way of natural
resources, so rulers had developed states

00:36:20.080 --> 00:36:22.770
reliant on mass piracy.

00:36:22.770 --> 00:36:27.360
Though functionally quite independent, the
Barbary States fell under the nominal control

00:36:27.360 --> 00:36:32.890
of the mighty Ottoman Empire, which spanned
from Greece to Africa.

00:36:32.890 --> 00:36:38.230
Barbary privateers could be called upon by
the Ottomans in times of war, and Dragut’s

00:36:38.230 --> 00:36:43.360
privateering had earned him prestige among
Ottoman leadership—most Barbary rulers were,

00:36:43.360 --> 00:36:45.440
in fact, corsairs themselves.

00:36:45.440 --> 00:36:50.440
But today, Dragut possessed neither power
nor pride.

00:36:50.440 --> 00:36:56.930
He had been captured by Italians after a bloody
raid and condemned to the oar: a backbreaking

00:36:56.930 --> 00:36:59.250
life aboard an Italian vessel.

00:36:59.250 --> 00:37:05.050
In the sweltering belly of the ship, Dragut
heaved, and pushed and pulled the oars day

00:37:05.050 --> 00:37:06.780
after day.

00:37:06.780 --> 00:37:10.820
His wiry muscles ached and his throat grew
parched.

00:37:10.820 --> 00:37:15.700
And so, the once notorious pirate festered
in obscurity.

00:37:15.700 --> 00:37:21.440
The man who had stolen and kidnapped countless
goods and people, now appeared destined to

00:37:21.440 --> 00:37:25.082
die a slave.

00:37:27.700 --> 00:37:31.380
Stripped of Dragut’s prowess, the Barbary
pirates looked vulnerable.

00:37:31.380 --> 00:37:36.700
At this time, Charles V reigned over much
of Europe, and with the blessing of the Pope,

00:37:36.700 --> 00:37:40.900
he called upon the nations of Christendom
to sail out against the Ottoman-backed Barbary

00:37:40.900 --> 00:37:42.100
pirates.

00:37:42.100 --> 00:37:44.900
To challenge the Ottomans was a dangerous
proposition.

00:37:44.900 --> 00:37:49.450
It was, after all, Ottoman overland hegemony
that had initially compelled Europeans like

00:37:49.450 --> 00:37:54.330
Columbus and Vasco da Gama to seek alternative
trade routes to Asia.

00:37:54.330 --> 00:37:59.830
Nobody appreciated that fact more than one
aging conqueror who had joined the fleet.

00:37:59.830 --> 00:38:06.630
Hernan Cortes peered out from a weather-beaten
face, wreathed by a beard that had grown gray.

00:38:06.630 --> 00:38:12.020
The man derided as a cruel tyrant by many
in Central America, had returned to the seas

00:38:12.020 --> 00:38:17.960
of the Old World to challenge the Barbary
corsairs and, perhaps, to reclaim some of

00:38:17.960 --> 00:38:22.320
the honor and glory to which he felt so entitled.

00:38:22.320 --> 00:38:27.800
The fleet set out in 1541, to take back the
Mediterranean under the command of the Genoese

00:38:27.800 --> 00:38:29.760
statesman Andrea Doria.

00:38:29.760 --> 00:38:34.920
But terrible storms beset the expedition and
tossed about the invading ships.

00:38:34.920 --> 00:38:38.960
Cortes nearly drowned, Doria lost nearly 150
vessels.

00:38:38.960 --> 00:38:45.650
When at last the remaining soldiers made landfall,
the locals massacred some, and sold so many

00:38:45.650 --> 00:38:50.330
of the survivors into slavery
it was said the price of a Christian head

00:38:50.330 --> 00:38:53.610
became equal to that of an onion.

00:38:53.610 --> 00:38:58.900
In the wake of the failed attack, the Ottomans
enjoyed unprecedented control over the Mediterranean.

00:38:58.900 --> 00:39:03.960
Under the cover of drooping tents, the markets
of the Barbary coast every day bustled with

00:39:03.960 --> 00:39:10.090
the fruitful exchange of goods and people—stolen
from passing ships or the coasts of West Africa

00:39:10.090 --> 00:39:11.560
and Europe.

00:39:11.560 --> 00:39:17.720
But even in the wake of victory, the Barbary
States felt the absence of Dragut.

00:39:17.720 --> 00:39:21.010
He still sat behind rusty bars in Genoa.

00:39:21.010 --> 00:39:25.590
The Ottomans eventually surrounded the city
and threatened to lay siege to it, unless

00:39:25.590 --> 00:39:27.240
Dragut was set free.

00:39:27.240 --> 00:39:29.340
The wary Genoese refused.

00:39:29.340 --> 00:39:32.520
The Ottomans paid a fortune in ransom money.

00:39:32.520 --> 00:39:37.170
Although years in slavery had drained Dragut,
freedom proved invigorating.

00:39:37.170 --> 00:39:39.420
He returned to the seas with ferocity.

00:39:39.420 --> 00:39:45.150
In 1546, the Ottoman Sultan named him the
commander of the Ottoman fleet and Bey of

00:39:45.150 --> 00:39:46.150
Algiers.

00:39:46.150 --> 00:39:51.140
As the de facto leader of the Barbary corsairs,
Dragut wrested control of Tripoli from the

00:39:51.140 --> 00:39:55.840
Knights of St. John and expelled European
incursions on the Barbary coast.

00:39:55.840 --> 00:40:01.570
And so, In 1560, Andrea Doria planned to destroy
his rival again.

00:40:01.570 --> 00:40:07.820
His ships sailed out to swarm Dragut, but
in the Battle of Djerba, Dragut decimated

00:40:07.820 --> 00:40:08.980
their fleet.

00:40:08.980 --> 00:40:14.880
Dragut and corsairs pulled the Spanish from
their ships, removed their heads, and stacked

00:40:14.880 --> 00:40:17.833
their skulls into a pyramid.

00:40:23.069 --> 00:40:28.120
Dragut had overseen the apex of Ottoman control
in the Mediterranean.

00:40:28.120 --> 00:40:31.620
He died in battle a few years later.

00:40:31.620 --> 00:40:35.450
The Barbary States he left behind were destined
to change.

00:40:35.450 --> 00:40:39.870
Burgeoning English and Dutch naval powers
pushed the Barbary corsairs into a tenuous

00:40:39.870 --> 00:40:41.940
peace in the 17th century.

00:40:41.940 --> 00:40:46.100
The Barbary States were forced to move away
from large scale attacks on shipping in the

00:40:46.100 --> 00:40:52.051
Mediterranean, and shifted their focus instead
to slavery.

00:40:56.340 --> 00:41:00.830
And so now we return to the Atlantic.

00:41:00.830 --> 00:41:06.700
The quiet seaside village of Baltimore sat
where the rocky coast meets verdant Irish

00:41:06.700 --> 00:41:08.160
hills.

00:41:08.160 --> 00:41:14.740
Squinting from the shore, villagers noticed
strange sails one day in 1631.

00:41:14.740 --> 00:41:18.550
Armed Barbary pirates disembarked.

00:41:18.550 --> 00:41:23.300
Baltimore was left a ghost town— little
more than shattered windows and the scattered

00:41:23.300 --> 00:41:25.150
belongings of missing people.

00:41:25.150 --> 00:41:31.050
Every man, woman, and child who hadn’t managed
to flee was brought in chains to the Barbary

00:41:31.050 --> 00:41:32.050
coast.

00:41:32.050 --> 00:41:36.670
A priest watched their arrival in the slave
market, “It was a piteous sight to see them

00:41:36.670 --> 00:41:40.970
exposed for sale at Algiers, for when they
parted the wife from the husband, and the

00:41:40.970 --> 00:41:46.720
father from the child; then, say I, they sell
the husband here, and the wife there, tearing

00:41:46.720 --> 00:41:54.150
from her arms the daughter whom she cannot
hope to see ever again.”

00:41:54.150 --> 00:41:58.510
Enslaved men were likely to spend their years
as galley slaves, while women and children

00:41:58.510 --> 00:42:01.180
were liable to be forced into concubinage.

00:42:01.180 --> 00:42:07.920
And as Europeans would soon come to appreciate,
slave trading paid handsomely.

00:42:07.920 --> 00:42:12.940
Crossing the Atlantic, the Spanish enslaved
indigenous people to extract wealth from American

00:42:12.940 --> 00:42:14.010
soil.

00:42:14.010 --> 00:42:18.370
This new land and wealth soon attracted rival
colonial powers.

00:42:18.370 --> 00:42:23.220
While the Spanish claimed Central and most
of South America, and Portugal claimed Brazil,

00:42:23.220 --> 00:42:29.640
the English, Dutch, and French began to encroach
and establish colonies in North America.

00:42:29.640 --> 00:42:35.970
If the Old World’s age of mass privateering
had seen its heyday under Dragut, the New

00:42:35.970 --> 00:42:42.890
World’s golden age of privateering had just
begun.

00:42:42.890 --> 00:42:50.530
L’olonnais would squeeze out the eyes, burn
the flesh, and remove the tongues of those

00:42:50.530 --> 00:42:53.080
who refused to tell him what he wanted to
hear.

00:42:53.080 --> 00:42:57.930
Now, L'olonnais demanded that the captured
Spaniard tell him the truth: whether or not

00:42:57.930 --> 00:43:02.990
he knew a safe, unguarded path to the city
of San Pedro.

00:43:02.990 --> 00:43:07.600
The Spaniard undoubtedly sensed that the Frenchman
planned to raid the city and torture from

00:43:07.600 --> 00:43:10.910
its civilians every ounce of wealth they possessed.

00:43:10.910 --> 00:43:15.300
The Spaniard told L'olonnais that he knew
of no such way to San Pedro.

00:43:15.300 --> 00:43:19.400
The story continues:
“Having asked them all, and finding they

00:43:19.400 --> 00:43:24.800
could show him no other way, Lolonois grew
outrageously passionate; so that he drew his

00:43:24.800 --> 00:43:28.860
cutlass, and with it cut open the breast of
one of those poor Spaniards, and pulling out

00:43:28.860 --> 00:43:34.770
his heart began to bite and gnaw it with his
teeth, like a ravenous wolf, saying to the

00:43:34.770 --> 00:43:38.810
rest, "I will serve you all alike, if you
show me not another way."

00:43:38.810 --> 00:43:44.900
Hereupon, those miserable wretches promised
to show him another way.”

00:43:44.900 --> 00:43:49.420
It was cold cruelty that had lifted L'olonnais
from the misery of indentured servitude to

00:43:49.420 --> 00:43:51.550
the heights of pirate leadership.

00:43:51.550 --> 00:43:56.170
He had captured treasure ships and pillaged
towns, all the while attracting followers

00:43:56.170 --> 00:43:57.500
to his cause.

00:43:57.500 --> 00:44:03.750
Like other so-called buccaneers, he operated
out of the neglected island of Tortuga.

00:44:03.750 --> 00:44:08.480
Over the years, his fleet of one ramshackle
ship had swelled to eight.

00:44:08.480 --> 00:44:11.660
But now, the Spanish looked prepared.

00:44:11.660 --> 00:44:15.270
When he reached San Pedro, they repelled his
attack.

00:44:15.270 --> 00:44:16.940
Fortune continued to turn against him.

00:44:16.940 --> 00:44:20.650
He sailed along the Central American coast,
and his ship ran aground.

00:44:20.650 --> 00:44:24.900
“To this effect he determined to go on to
the coasts of Carthagena; but God Almighty,

00:44:24.900 --> 00:44:30.860
the time of His Divine justice being now come,
had appointed the Indians of Darien to be

00:44:30.860 --> 00:44:33.560
the instruments and executioners thereof...

00:44:33.560 --> 00:44:39.080
Hither Lolonois came (brought by his evil
conscience that cried for punishment), thinking

00:44:39.080 --> 00:44:44.480
to act his cruelties; but the Indians within
a few days after his arrival took him prisoner,

00:44:44.480 --> 00:44:50.610
and tore him in pieces alive, throwing his
body limb by limb into the fire, and his ashes

00:44:50.610 --> 00:44:59.960
into the air, that no trace or memory might
remain of such an infamous, inhuman creature.”

00:44:59.960 --> 00:45:02.250
Contemporaries of L'olonnais judged him a
wicked man.

00:45:02.250 --> 00:45:07.000
But his rise had been made possible by the
unique conditions brought about by rivalries

00:45:07.000 --> 00:45:12.420
among overstretched empires, and nurtured
in the backwater regions to which Spain tenuously

00:45:12.420 --> 00:45:13.850
laid claim.

00:45:13.850 --> 00:45:26.470
L'olonnais was part of a growing class of
pirate, known in this age as buccaneers...

00:45:26.470 --> 00:45:30.400
Buccaneers had always existed in defiance
of the Spanish Crown, but they hadn’t always

00:45:30.400 --> 00:45:31.820
been pirates.

00:45:31.820 --> 00:45:36.190
They had gotten their start as poor settlers
who ventured into the overgrown north end

00:45:36.190 --> 00:45:40.110
of Spanish Hispaniola to rustle feral cattle
and pigs.

00:45:40.110 --> 00:45:44.600
The forest-dwellers sold this meat to English,
Dutch, and French merchants in exchange for

00:45:44.600 --> 00:45:46.710
weapons, ammunition, and rum.

00:45:46.710 --> 00:45:51.060
Since Hispaniola's native Taino people had
traditionally cooked meat on wooden frames

00:45:51.060 --> 00:45:55.910
called buccan, the French and English took
to calling these meat smugglers boucaniers

00:45:55.910 --> 00:45:58.360
or buccaneers.

00:45:58.360 --> 00:46:04.090
The Spanish, meanwhile, referred to these
English, Dutch and French merchants as “interlopers,”.

00:46:04.090 --> 00:46:08.820
To safeguard their monopoly and ward them
off, they drove the buccaneers from Hispaniola

00:46:08.820 --> 00:46:11.370
and decimated the animals they hunted.

00:46:11.370 --> 00:46:17.150
In doing so, the Spanish inadvertently created
a pirate haven.

00:46:17.150 --> 00:46:23.580
The island of Tortuga: a tropical paradise
where, from the 1630s onward, disaffected

00:46:23.580 --> 00:46:29.020
smugglers could carouse with French, English,
and Dutch interlopers and build a Caribbean

00:46:29.020 --> 00:46:30.280
black market.

00:46:30.280 --> 00:46:33.960
Soon, these men of Tortuga turned to piracy.

00:46:33.960 --> 00:46:39.070
The buccaneers began to sail out on small
boats in the dead of night to ambush Spanish

00:46:39.070 --> 00:46:40.540
trade ships.

00:46:40.540 --> 00:46:44.730
Around this time, the French and Dutch arose
as commercial and naval rivals to the Spanish

00:46:44.730 --> 00:46:50.220
in the 30 Years’ War, which pitted the Catholic
Habsburg rulers of Spain and Austria against

00:46:50.220 --> 00:46:54.250
the Dutch Republic and the rival Bourbon house
of France.

00:46:54.250 --> 00:46:58.370
Spain’s grasp on the New World was loosening.

00:46:58.370 --> 00:47:02.450
The English established colonies across the
North American East coast; and the French

00:47:02.450 --> 00:47:04.970
began to venture from Canada to Louisiana.

00:47:04.970 --> 00:47:11.250
But despite a dizzying succession of 17th-century
wars between European powers, the politics

00:47:11.250 --> 00:47:14.850
in Caribbean waters were refreshingly simple.

00:47:14.850 --> 00:47:17.750
The Spanish were the enemy.

00:47:17.750 --> 00:47:21.260
Regardless of the goings on in Europe, English,
Dutch, and French governors in the West Indies

00:47:21.260 --> 00:47:23.090
had made their choice.

00:47:23.090 --> 00:47:27.820
Colonial authorities weaponized the buccaneers
of Tortuga with letters of marque, and let

00:47:27.820 --> 00:47:33.345
them loose on the Spanish.

00:47:37.300 --> 00:47:40.730
The buccaneers of Tortuga smelled blood in
the water.

00:47:40.730 --> 00:47:44.920
They raided the cities along the Spanish Main
and Spain’s merchant ships.

00:47:44.920 --> 00:47:48.590
Africans, escaped slaves, and outlaws also
joined their ranks.

00:47:48.590 --> 00:47:54.230
In spite of the sadism practiced by buccaneers
like L’olonnais, these self-titled “Brethren

00:47:54.230 --> 00:47:59.920
of the Coast” generally boasted a commonwealth
spirit, whereby decisions were made collectively,

00:47:59.920 --> 00:48:06.260
resources were pooled together, and loot was
divided according to well-understood parameters.

00:48:06.260 --> 00:48:11.750
Like the English Sea Dogs of Francis Drake’s
time or the Barbary pirates, buccaneers toed

00:48:11.750 --> 00:48:16.600
the line between roguish outlaws and agents
of the state.

00:48:16.600 --> 00:48:23.530
When war reached Tortuga, it was English colonial
authorities who graciously provided a new

00:48:23.530 --> 00:48:25.510
buccaneer haven.

00:48:25.510 --> 00:48:31.260
England had taken Jamaica in 1655, and its
city of Port Royal now provided implicit support

00:48:31.260 --> 00:48:33.920
for these anti-Spanish privateers.

00:48:33.920 --> 00:48:39.570
Under the regal banner of England, the buccaneers
arrived in ships stocked with plundered rum,

00:48:39.570 --> 00:48:42.030
slaves, cloth, and cash crops.

00:48:42.030 --> 00:48:48.080
The ensuing trade facilitated the scandalous
metamorphosis of Port Royal into the so-called

00:48:48.080 --> 00:48:50.990
“Sodom of the New World.”

00:48:50.990 --> 00:48:57.000
Here, a buccaneer could squander his Spanish
loot, cheer and brawl in a muggy tavern, or

00:48:57.000 --> 00:49:00.450
unwind on the warped mattress of a busy brothel.

00:49:00.450 --> 00:49:05.130
When one clergyman retired from his attempts
to preach to the inhabitants of Port Royal,

00:49:05.130 --> 00:49:12.290
he remarked, “the majority of its population
consists of pirates, cutthroats, whores and

00:49:12.290 --> 00:49:18.550
some of the vilest persons in the whole of
the world.”

00:49:18.550 --> 00:49:24.600
Port Royal became a blackmarket boomtown that
rivaled the upright prosperity of England’s

00:49:24.600 --> 00:49:26.310
Puritan Boston.

00:49:26.310 --> 00:49:32.650
Tortuga, meanwhile, continued to serve as
a neutral port for buccaneers.

00:49:32.650 --> 00:49:40.580
It was into this world of opportunity that
Henry Morgan sailed in the early 1660s.

00:49:40.580 --> 00:49:47.300
The sailor, in his early thirties, had cut
his teeth as a Welsh privateer sacking Spanish

00:49:47.300 --> 00:49:48.300
cities.

00:49:48.300 --> 00:49:52.440
Steady, deep-set brown eyes betrayed a cunning
self-assuredness.

00:49:52.440 --> 00:49:59.250
He had trained under powerful buccaneers,
but the time had come to build his own legacy.

00:49:59.250 --> 00:50:02.710
Anchoring in Port Royal, he flaunted his English
letter of marque.

00:50:02.710 --> 00:50:07.630
It authorized attacks on Spanish shipping,
and unsavory sailors wallowing in the town’s

00:50:07.630 --> 00:50:09.530
taverns were keen to take advantage.

00:50:09.530 --> 00:50:15.410
12 ships and 500 English and Frenchmen sailed
out under Morgan’s command.

00:50:15.410 --> 00:50:23.960
In a matter of days, Morgan had become the
de facto king of the buccaneers.

00:50:23.960 --> 00:50:30.310
The invasion fleet dropped anchor on the Haitian
coast.

00:50:30.310 --> 00:50:31.310
In truth, letters of marque only licensed
the seizure of enemy vessels on the high seas

00:50:31.310 --> 00:50:32.310
- but Morgan knew his countrymen in Port Royal
were likely to turn a blind eye to any profitable

00:50:32.310 --> 00:50:33.310
misstep he might make.

00:50:33.310 --> 00:50:34.310
From the Haitian beach, Morgan rallied the
buccaneers and stormed the city of Puerto

00:50:34.310 --> 00:50:35.310
del Principe.

00:50:35.310 --> 00:50:39.820
They killed hundreds of Spanish defenders
and pillaged for three days, but their earnings

00:50:39.820 --> 00:50:41.610
were insufficient.

00:50:41.610 --> 00:50:46.920
The buccaneers moaned and cursed - until Morgan
offered up an idea: they would move on to

00:50:46.920 --> 00:50:51.340
the Panamanian coast and attack the city of
Portobelo.

00:50:51.340 --> 00:50:56.100
Stocked full of shimmering Spanish treasure,
but crawling with well-trained militiamen,

00:50:56.100 --> 00:50:58.930
Portobelo was a dangerous prize.

00:50:58.930 --> 00:51:03.380
The French, who had followed Morgan thus far,
balked at the idea and sailed off.

00:51:03.380 --> 00:51:08.450
Captain Morgan remarked, "If our number is
small, our hearts are great; and the fewer

00:51:08.450 --> 00:51:18.560
persons we are, the more union and better
shares we shall have in the spoil."

00:51:18.560 --> 00:51:23.600
The unwavering Morgan roused the remaining
crew and guided his fleet quietly through

00:51:23.600 --> 00:51:25.140
the Bocas del Toro.

00:51:25.140 --> 00:51:28.450
They transferred into smaller vessels when
they reached the coast.

00:51:28.450 --> 00:51:32.840
The weary pirates staggered onto the beach
and marched toward Portobelo.

00:51:32.840 --> 00:51:35.530
At daybreak, they charged.

00:51:35.530 --> 00:51:38.150
The morning silence was split by musket fire.

00:51:38.150 --> 00:51:41.890
Morgan and his men advanced in a haze of blackpowder.

00:51:41.890 --> 00:51:44.150
Women screamed and children fled.

00:51:44.150 --> 00:51:47.970
The buccaneers fired at every living thing
in sight.

00:51:47.970 --> 00:51:54.800
Morgan overran the city, but the precious
citadel of Fort Santiago still loomed in the

00:51:54.800 --> 00:51:55.800
distance.

00:51:55.800 --> 00:51:59.060
The Spanish militia atop its walls rained
down stones and explosive pots on all who

00:51:59.060 --> 00:52:00.600
neared the fort.

00:52:00.600 --> 00:52:03.900
Morgan pulled back and adjusted his strategy.

00:52:03.900 --> 00:52:09.490
He readied siege ladders and corralled the
city’s nuns and friars, forcing them to

00:52:09.490 --> 00:52:12.500
march forth in a human shield.

00:52:12.500 --> 00:52:17.371
“Thus the ladders...were put into the hands
of religious persons of both sexes, and these

00:52:17.371 --> 00:52:21.780
were forced, at the head of the companies,
to raise and apply them to the walls…”

00:52:21.780 --> 00:52:26.800
“Thus many of the religious men and nuns
were killed before they could fix the ladders;

00:52:26.800 --> 00:52:31.790
which at last being done, though with great
loss of the said religious people, the pirates

00:52:31.790 --> 00:52:36.230
mounted them in great numbers...being now
at the top of the walls, they kindled and

00:52:36.230 --> 00:52:40.540
cast in among the Spaniards.“
They buccaneers killed the governor in front

00:52:40.540 --> 00:52:45.540
of his own wife and daughter, and wounded
or killed every other Spaniard therein.

00:52:45.540 --> 00:52:49.089
With Portobelo hostage, Henry Morgan sensed
an opportunity.

00:52:49.089 --> 00:52:54.470
He sent a letter to the governor of Panama:
“Tomorrow we plan to burn this city to the

00:52:54.470 --> 00:52:59.120
ground and then set sail with all the guns
and munitions from the castles.

00:52:59.120 --> 00:53:03.790
With us we will take all our prisoners….and
we will show them the same kindness that the

00:53:03.790 --> 00:53:07.720
English prisoners have received in this place.”

00:53:07.720 --> 00:53:11.790
Morgan added that he would spare the city
of this fate if the governor promptly paid

00:53:11.790 --> 00:53:16.150
him 350,000 pieces-of-eight.

00:53:16.150 --> 00:53:20.930
From their conquered forts, Morgan’s buccaneers
repulsed the Panamanian counterattack, negotiated

00:53:20.930 --> 00:53:24.930
a ransom for 100,000 pieces-of-eight, and
sailed off.

00:53:24.930 --> 00:53:30.680
However news of changing diplomatic tides
met them on the docks, and spoiled their homecoming.

00:53:30.680 --> 00:53:35.960
It appeared the age of the buccaneer was coming
to a close.

00:53:35.960 --> 00:53:40.750
England had adopted friendlier relations with
Spain and the Crown now rebuked this wave

00:53:40.750 --> 00:53:42.290
of piracy.

00:53:42.290 --> 00:53:44.900
Henry Morgan was forced to retire.

00:53:44.900 --> 00:53:48.970
But not for long.

00:53:48.970 --> 00:53:52.560
Global communication in those days was fraught
with delay.

00:53:52.560 --> 00:53:57.250
Local Spanish authorities, ignorant of the
new truce, sent out privateers to exact revenge

00:53:57.250 --> 00:53:59.460
on the English for the Portobelo raid.

00:53:59.460 --> 00:54:04.450
Their hostility, in turn, provided Morgan
with an opportunity to return to the seas

00:54:04.450 --> 00:54:11.120
and fulfill an earlier promise: to sack the
most prized metropolis in the Americas: Panama

00:54:11.120 --> 00:54:13.930
City.

00:54:13.930 --> 00:54:19.230
In early 1671, Morgan readied the buccaneers
for his greatest plan yet.

00:54:19.230 --> 00:54:25.350
They overran the city, scavenged all the wealth
they could find, brutalized the inhabitants,

00:54:25.350 --> 00:54:27.890
and burned it to the ground.

00:54:27.890 --> 00:54:33.350
Morgan was arrested for piracy and transported
to London in 1672 for punishment, but by the

00:54:33.350 --> 00:54:39.430
time he had arrived the diplomatic winds had
turned again.

00:54:39.430 --> 00:54:43.580
Spain had elected to support the Dutch in
the Third Anglo-Dutch War, and was therefore

00:54:43.580 --> 00:54:46.270
again an enemy of England.

00:54:46.270 --> 00:54:52.050
Morgan walked free, while throngs of commoners
and royalty alike hailed him as a national

00:54:52.050 --> 00:55:00.060
hero who had ravaged the hated Spanish.

00:55:00.060 --> 00:55:04.950
In the coming decades, rarer and stricter
letters of marque saw less cooperation between

00:55:04.950 --> 00:55:10.340
buccaneers of different nations, and eventually
fewer buccaneers altogether.

00:55:10.340 --> 00:55:15.860
The reign of the buccaneers ended not in glorious
battle, but in stuffy courtrooms and royal

00:55:15.860 --> 00:55:16.860
negotiations.

00:55:16.860 --> 00:55:17.860
Disaffected would-be privateers soon left
the West to partake in the so-called Pirate

00:55:17.860 --> 00:55:18.860
Round.

00:55:18.860 --> 00:55:19.860
Men like Thomas Tew, William Kidd, and Henry
Avery ventured to the coasts of Africa, setting

00:55:19.860 --> 00:55:21.150
up base on Madagascar to pillage British East
India and Mughal shipping, and harass Muslim

00:55:21.150 --> 00:55:22.150
pilgrimages.

00:55:22.150 --> 00:55:27.080
By the time of his death in 1688, the gentrified
Henry Morgan had turned his piratical earnings

00:55:27.080 --> 00:55:33.470
into several profitable plantations—raked
and picked by over a hundred miserable slaves.

00:55:33.470 --> 00:55:38.620
It was said that Morgan gorged himself on
food and drink in his final years.

00:55:38.620 --> 00:55:43.520
Privateers like himself had maintained the
diplomatic veneer of national service; they

00:55:43.520 --> 00:55:49.050
attacked and stole within recognized, if often
neglected, parameters set by colonial authorities.

00:55:49.050 --> 00:55:53.870
The next generation of pirates would dispense
with such niceties.

00:55:53.870 --> 00:55:58.710
Unshackled by diplomacy and unburdened of
the weight of nation, the men of the black

00:55:58.710 --> 00:56:01.090
flag answered to no one.

00:56:01.090 --> 00:56:03.720
They knelt before no king.

00:56:03.720 --> 00:56:08.399
The Caribbean was at their mercy.

00:56:13.357 --> 00:56:17.280
A dozen sails rippled above panicked crews.

00:56:17.280 --> 00:56:21.570
Hundreds of cannons had been employed to ensure
the safety of this twelve-ship flota.

00:56:21.570 --> 00:56:26.500
The Spanish were doing what they had done
for more than a century - heaving gold home

00:56:26.500 --> 00:56:27.670
from the New World.

00:56:27.670 --> 00:56:33.890
But on this occasion, the figure that captured
the horizon wasn’t a black flag, but a dark

00:56:33.890 --> 00:56:35.610
tempest.

00:56:35.610 --> 00:56:37.750
Dread overcame the sailors.

00:56:37.750 --> 00:56:40.190
It was too late to escape.

00:56:40.190 --> 00:56:45.430
Black clouds flooded the skies, and furious
seas took hold of the ships.

00:56:45.430 --> 00:56:48.910
Desperate men clung to the masts, which tore
asunder.

00:56:48.910 --> 00:56:52.950
Sails flew into the deluge, sailors tumbled
into the void.

00:56:52.950 --> 00:56:57.400
One galleon was thrown against the reef; its
hulking shadow sunk into the depths, with

00:56:57.400 --> 00:57:01.760
a drowned crew left to rot in the dark of
its cabin.

00:57:01.760 --> 00:57:10.240
11 of the 12 vessels suffered the same fate.

00:57:10.240 --> 00:57:15.660
In the morning, warm sunlight penetrated the
clouds and shimmered on calm seas.

00:57:15.660 --> 00:57:18.500
The storm had claimed over a thousand men.

00:57:18.500 --> 00:57:24.521
But Scattered amongst their drifting corpses
were precious metals, fine silks - and seven

00:57:24.521 --> 00:57:28.470
million pieces-of-eight-worth of South American
treasure.

00:57:28.470 --> 00:57:37.570
This fortune had been slated to sustain the
Empire for the next year - The Spanish at

00:57:37.570 --> 00:57:43.020
Havana immediately sent out an expedition
to recover it, but word of the wreck had gotten

00:57:43.020 --> 00:57:45.960
out...

00:57:45.960 --> 00:57:52.730
“Captain Henry Jennings, sail’d to the
Gulf, and found the Spaniards there upon the

00:57:52.730 --> 00:57:57.660
Wreck; the Money before spoken of, was left
on Shore, deposited in a Store-House.

00:57:57.660 --> 00:58:02.700
The Rovers came directly upon the Place, bringing
their little Fleet to an Anchor, and, in a

00:58:02.700 --> 00:58:07.070
Word, landing 300 Men, they attack’d the
Guard, who immediately ran away; and thus

00:58:07.070 --> 00:58:12.980
they seized the Treasure, which they carried
off, making the best of their Way to Jamaica.”

00:58:12.980 --> 00:58:17.470
Among the ne’er-do-wells of Port Royal,
Charles Vane had appeared to cut a thoroughly

00:58:17.470 --> 00:58:23.120
unremarkable figure - but he had ambition
enough to join his more diplomatic friend,

00:58:23.120 --> 00:58:28.300
the former privateer Henry Jennings, who had
managed to cobble together a crew.

00:58:28.300 --> 00:58:32.610
The gentlemanly Jennings had intended to stop
the Spanish salvage expedition and steal as

00:58:32.610 --> 00:58:36.990
much treasure from the wreckage as he could
- and together that they did.

00:58:36.990 --> 00:58:41.970
On their way home from their successful expedition,
Jennings and Vane encountered a Spanish ship.

00:58:41.970 --> 00:58:46.500
They raided it too, stole the treasure aboard,
and went on their way.

00:58:46.500 --> 00:58:49.320
But selling their loot was complicated.

00:58:49.320 --> 00:58:52.650
The world had turned against the pirate.

00:58:52.650 --> 00:58:58.640
Gone were the days of unrestrained buccaneering
against the Spanish— this was peacetime,

00:58:58.640 --> 00:59:03.470
and London saw to it that its British colonies
disavowed Jennings and the other pirates.

00:59:03.470 --> 00:59:08.290
During the War of Spanish Succession, England,
the Dutch Republic, and Portugal had rallied

00:59:08.290 --> 00:59:10.930
against France.

00:59:10.930 --> 00:59:18.500
Bloodshed and conquest had returned to the
Caribbean, and the privateers roamed free.

00:59:18.500 --> 00:59:23.790
But now, the winds had turned yet again and
the debaucherous doors of Port Royal were

00:59:23.790 --> 00:59:30.520
closed to stolen goods and maritime outlaws.

00:59:30.520 --> 00:59:34.840
If men like Henry Jennings and Charles Vane
were going to become pirates, they would need

00:59:34.840 --> 00:59:39.270
friendly ports and a base of operations.

00:59:39.270 --> 00:59:44.530
Only a few years removed from war, the Bahamian
island of New Providence, and its city of

00:59:44.530 --> 00:59:48.060
Nassau, sat scarred and nearly abandoned.

00:59:48.060 --> 00:59:52.850
Green and red-throated parrots warbled above
decaying forts, which hadn’t flown British

00:59:52.850 --> 00:59:54.960
colors in years.

00:59:54.960 --> 00:59:59.080
Frothy waves lapped against white shores and
leaning palm trees.

00:59:59.080 --> 01:00:03.740
Nassau also boasted a natural harbor that
could hold a hundred ships, located near trade

01:00:03.740 --> 01:00:06.780
routes and the wreckage of the treasure fleet.

01:00:06.780 --> 01:00:13.200
This was the scene the former privateer Benjamin
Hornigold stumbled upon: an Edenic island

01:00:13.200 --> 01:00:16.150
bereft of any semblance of governance.

01:00:16.150 --> 01:00:20.490
Hornigold quickly assumed control and gave
the pirates exactly what they needed.

01:00:20.490 --> 01:00:25.030
Jennings, Vane, and the thousands of other
disaffected sailors were welcomed into the

01:00:25.030 --> 01:00:27.970
pirate’s paradise of Nassau.

01:00:27.970 --> 01:00:32.520
The pirates clambered ashore with pistols
and swords, and faces freckled with scurvy-spots—-

01:00:32.520 --> 01:00:38.660
here, they spent their ill-gotten wealth on
rolls of dice, rounds of rum, and seedy nights

01:00:38.660 --> 01:00:40.330
in rundown brothels.

01:00:40.330 --> 01:00:44.980
Chests of immaculate gold and silver from
the wreckage made their way into town.

01:00:44.980 --> 01:00:50.280
In the throes of treasure fever, the pirates
indulged in lawless excess.

01:00:50.280 --> 01:00:55.230
From the fortified port of Nassau, captains
could launch attacks on passing merchant vessels.

01:00:55.230 --> 01:01:00.160
“In the Commonwealth of Pyrates, he who
goes the greatest Length of Wickedness, is

01:01:00.160 --> 01:01:06.150
looked upon with a kind of Envy amongst them,
as a Person of a more extraordinary Gallantry,

01:01:06.150 --> 01:01:12.030
and is thereby entitled to be distinguished
by some Post, and if such a one has but Courage,

01:01:12.030 --> 01:01:16.010
he must certainly be a great Man.”

01:01:16.010 --> 01:01:21.630
The pirates attacked merchant ships, hauling
in tobacco, rum, cotton, tar, sugar, and slaves

01:01:21.630 --> 01:01:24.790
before returning to the safety of Nassau.

01:01:24.790 --> 01:01:28.280
Englishmen attacked Englishmen without regard
for common heritage.

01:01:28.280 --> 01:01:32.970
Bold brutality became a badge of pride.

01:01:32.970 --> 01:01:42.270
For all its anarchical trappings, Nassau came
to be governed, in a sense, by two rival pirate

01:01:42.270 --> 01:01:47.730
factions, one under Henry Jennings and the
other under Benjamin Hornigold.

01:01:47.730 --> 01:01:54.490
Hornigold’s rising second-in-command, Edward
Teach or Blackbeard, also held sway.

01:01:54.490 --> 01:01:58.660
Even so, top-down tyranny had no place in
Nassau.

01:01:58.660 --> 01:02:03.420
In a period when monarchies were widespread,
and common sailors languished under draconian

01:02:03.420 --> 01:02:11.390
captains for poor wages, this so-called Republic
of Pirates embraced fraternity and proto-egalitarianism

01:02:11.390 --> 01:02:14.070
in its undaunting pursuit of wealth.

01:02:14.070 --> 01:02:19.401
Half a century before the eloquent democratic
treatises of Paine and Jefferson, the pirate

01:02:19.401 --> 01:02:25.100
ship became a primordial site of defiance
toward absolutism and monarchy.

01:02:25.100 --> 01:02:31.380
Indeed, the pirates of this era made decisions
collectively, voted out incompetent and unfair

01:02:31.380 --> 01:02:33.420
captains, and shared their loot.

01:02:33.420 --> 01:02:38.690
Many followed pirate codes that harshly penalized
disloyalty and the cheating of one’s own

01:02:38.690 --> 01:02:39.690
crew.

01:02:39.690 --> 01:02:44.670
While many still reserved harsh violence for
their victims, pirates like Blackbeard spurned

01:02:44.670 --> 01:02:51.660
violence deemed unnecessary, cultivating instead
an aura of fear to force victims into surrendering

01:02:51.660 --> 01:02:53.470
without resistance.

01:02:53.470 --> 01:02:58.850
The pirate flag, or Jolly Roger, was intended
to do the same.

01:02:58.850 --> 01:03:06.180
The red or black flag often flew emblazoned
with icons of death—skulls, bones, and hourglasses.

01:03:06.180 --> 01:03:10.980
Pirates used flags strategically; tailing
target vessels and hoisting the feared emblems

01:03:10.980 --> 01:03:21.258
of piracy when close enough to make good on
their implicit threat to unleash carnage.

01:03:21.258 --> 01:03:26.260
But by 1726, King George I had had enough.

01:03:26.260 --> 01:03:32.270
From the opulence of his palace, he made a
dramatic move to annihilate the pirate threat

01:03:32.270 --> 01:03:34.640
and conquer Nassau.

01:03:34.640 --> 01:03:39.910
His plan would fracture whatever unity these
pitiful enemies of the state professed.

01:03:39.910 --> 01:03:42.820
His Majesty had issued a royal pardon.

01:03:42.820 --> 01:03:46.810
In this carrot and stick approach, pirates
who surrendered to the proper authorities

01:03:46.810 --> 01:03:51.220
in the coming year, would be pardoned and
free to carry on with their lives.

01:03:51.220 --> 01:03:55.680
Those who rejected this proposal would, in
turn, receive a price on their head and a

01:03:55.680 --> 01:04:01.040
royal promise that they would be hunted down,
arrested, and killed.

01:04:01.040 --> 01:04:05.530
When news from New York of the impending pardon
reached the shores of Nassau, hundreds of

01:04:05.530 --> 01:04:07.720
pirates rejoiced.

01:04:07.720 --> 01:04:12.310
Henry Jennings and Benjamin Hornigold, and
many other former privateers had never intended

01:04:12.310 --> 01:04:14.400
to be reviled pirates.

01:04:14.400 --> 01:04:18.930
Profit had possessed them, but now they saw
a fortuitous escape to the world of respectable

01:04:18.930 --> 01:04:20.230
commerce.

01:04:20.230 --> 01:04:26.840
Amidst their euphoria, the pro-pardon pirates
restored the Union Jack atop the fort of Nassau.

01:04:26.840 --> 01:04:29.850
But there was a schism.

01:04:29.850 --> 01:04:34.710
Hundreds of men refused to return to the mundane
poverty of civility, those who embraced the

01:04:34.710 --> 01:04:39.800
life of a ship-borne outlaw and the bloody
quest for fortune and leisure.

01:04:39.800 --> 01:04:44.850
These pirates, including Edward England, Christopher
“Billy One-Hand” Condent, and “Calico”

01:04:44.850 --> 01:04:52.020
Jack Rackham, now found a leader in the most
diehard, anti-pardon pirate of them all: Charles

01:04:52.020 --> 01:04:54.180
Vane.

01:04:54.180 --> 01:04:58.510
Vane rallied his supporters and staged an
armed takeover of the fort.

01:04:58.510 --> 01:05:04.530
He tore down the Union Jack and raised high
the grinning face of death— the black flag.

01:05:04.530 --> 01:05:10.610
For the time being, Nassau would remain the
unapologetic property of pirates, a brazen

01:05:10.610 --> 01:05:14.810
island of liberty, chaos, and wealth.

01:05:14.810 --> 01:05:19.031
In their defiance of King George, Charles
Vane and his supporters even attempted to

01:05:19.031 --> 01:05:23.330
drum up support from the Jacobite cause, which
sought to restore the deposed House of Stuart

01:05:23.330 --> 01:05:24.710
upon the British throne.

01:05:24.710 --> 01:05:29.270
Although the bid was unsuccessful, tensions
in Nassau were rising.

01:05:29.270 --> 01:05:32.270
A pirate council was called in early 1718.

01:05:32.270 --> 01:05:36.960
Vane argued that they should fortify the island
and stand against the British.

01:05:36.960 --> 01:05:41.330
His old friend Henry Jennings disagreed; he
advocated taking the pardon.

01:05:41.330 --> 01:05:46.740
A great clamor and shouting came over the
room, and nothing was decided upon.

01:05:46.740 --> 01:05:53.770
The Republic of Pirates felt irrevocably fractured,
and the end appeared close.

01:05:53.770 --> 01:05:55.410
The exodus had begun.

01:05:55.410 --> 01:05:59.880
Henry Jennings and fifteen of his men sailed
to British Bermuda, and formally received

01:05:59.880 --> 01:06:01.200
pardons.

01:06:01.200 --> 01:06:06.190
Others fitted their ships for long piratical
journeys elsewhere, to South America or Africa.

01:06:06.190 --> 01:06:09.540
Blackbeard, meanwhile, had not been seen for
months.

01:06:09.540 --> 01:06:14.320
In February, the HMS Phoenix arrived in Nassau.

01:06:14.320 --> 01:06:18.300
Captain Vincent Pearse formally offered the
pirates the king’s pardon.

01:06:18.300 --> 01:06:22.381
Benjamin Hornigold and the pro-pardon pirates
happily accepted.

01:06:22.381 --> 01:06:24.608
They warned Pearse of the zealous Charles Vane,

01:06:24.608 --> 01:06:28.526
who was, at that very moment, outfitting a ship for piracy.

01:06:28.526 --> 01:06:33.997
Captain Pearse sailed around the island, and let loose a volley of cannon fire on Vane’s vessel.

01:06:33.997 --> 01:06:38.952
He arrested Charles Vane, but, in a show of good faith to the pirates, he released him.

01:06:38.952 --> 01:06:46.200
Vane and his crew accepted the king’s pardon with the promise that they would turn away from piracy.

01:06:46.200 --> 01:06:58.565
Pearse also informed them that the former buccaneer, Woodes Rogers, had been appointed governor of Nassau and would arrive in the summer to take the island and restore order.

01:06:58.565 --> 01:07:06.525
Vane accepted the pardon in the moment, but he hadnt the slightest intention of keeping his word.

01:07:06.525 --> 01:07:10.697
Rebellion grew.

01:07:12.377 --> 01:07:20.894
Charles Vane hoisted the black flag in defiant theatricality, and left Nassau to exact cruelties on nearby trade routes.

01:07:20.894 --> 01:07:30.118
For three weeks, Vane continued like this, hacking and hanging his victims. He captured a dozen merchant ships and crippled trade in the Bahamas.

01:07:30.118 --> 01:07:39.223
When he finally sailed back to Nassau, Vane was delighted to find that Pearse had departed to seek repairs for his damaged ship.

01:07:39.223 --> 01:07:47.200
A hulking five-vessel fleet with towering
warships approached and laid anchor... Blackbeard was in town.

01:07:47.200 --> 01:07:52.120
His 700-strong crew found their land legs
in the taverns and brothels of Nassau.

01:07:52.120 --> 01:07:58.200
For a moment, it looked like the glorious,
pre-pardon days of the island had returned.

01:07:58.200 --> 01:08:02.562
Blackbeard, however, was not keen on a potential
run-in with Woodes Rogers and the Crown..

01:08:02.562 --> 01:08:05.810
In a few days, he and his crew sailed out
again.

01:08:05.810 --> 01:08:10.280
Finally, Vane attacked Benjamin Hornigold
and proclaimed himself the governor of New

01:08:10.280 --> 01:08:11.430
Providence.

01:08:11.430 --> 01:08:16.640
It seemed Charles Vane was the last true pirate
lord in this corner of the world.

01:08:16.640 --> 01:08:20.679
Old allies had turned against him - Henry
Jennings had even accepted a privateering

01:08:20.679 --> 01:08:22.690
commission to arrest Vane.

01:08:22.690 --> 01:08:28.030
But Vane, rich with the spoils of his exploits,
now planned to set sail for Brazil with his

01:08:28.030 --> 01:08:32.529
crew— where they might regroup and build
another pirate paradise.

01:08:32.529 --> 01:08:37.330
But as they readied their ships, hostile sails
appeared.

01:08:37.330 --> 01:08:40.880
Woodes Rogers blocked Vane in the harbor.

01:08:40.880 --> 01:08:48.420
This was the final chance to take the pardon
and escape alive.

01:08:51.707 --> 01:08:56.279
“[A]ll surrendered to the Pardon, except
Charles Vane and his Crew, which happened

01:08:56.279 --> 01:09:01.470
after this Manner...the Harbour to [had] two
Inlets, by Means of a small Island lying at

01:09:01.470 --> 01:09:06.580
the Mouth of it; at one of which, both the
Men of War entered, and left the other open,

01:09:06.580 --> 01:09:12.080
so that Vane slip’d his Cable, set Fire
to a large Prize they had there, and resolutely

01:09:12.080 --> 01:09:17.900
put out, firing at the Man of War as he went
off.”

01:09:17.900 --> 01:09:22.900
Woodes Rogers nevertheless became the governor
of New Providence, and he entrusted Benjamin

01:09:22.900 --> 01:09:26.819
Hornigold with a dangerous mission to hunt
down Charles Vane.

01:09:26.819 --> 01:09:32.150
But Vane had informants and old allies scattered
across the West Indies, and he shrewdly evaded

01:09:32.150 --> 01:09:34.690
Rogers and Hornigold at every turn.

01:09:34.690 --> 01:09:40.080
As Charles Vane persisted in a bloody tour
of piracy, dozens of superficially reformed

01:09:40.080 --> 01:09:45.770
pirates betrayed the pardon, stole ships,
and sailed off to join his cause.

01:09:45.770 --> 01:09:51.880
If arrested, they would be tried under maritime
law, and thus afforded no jury.

01:09:51.880 --> 01:10:00.050
Pirate trials were little more than a show.

01:10:00.050 --> 01:10:04.070
Without the aid of a defense lawyer, the mostly
illiterate sea-thieves would be asked to prove

01:10:04.070 --> 01:10:07.170
their innocence to a panel of appointed officials.

01:10:07.170 --> 01:10:11.830
The accused would inevitably be found guilty,
hanged on the foreshore, and buried in an

01:10:11.830 --> 01:10:16.130
unmarked grave or left to rot in a swinging
cage.

01:10:16.130 --> 01:10:21.730
This was the fate the Crown now reserved for
all pirates.

01:10:21.730 --> 01:10:27.720
Charles Vane pressed onward.

01:10:27.720 --> 01:10:31.090
While many of his old crew— like Edward
England and Christopher Condent— had left

01:10:31.090 --> 01:10:36.550
for Africa and Brazil, one powerful pirate
still stalked American waters.

01:10:36.550 --> 01:10:41.450
Vane found Blackbeard in the Ocracoke Inlet,
off the coast of North Carolina, and ordered

01:10:41.450 --> 01:10:45.880
cannons fired in a salute to his fellow pirate.

01:10:45.880 --> 01:10:50.850
As the two crews fraternized and drank for
several nights on the beach, Vane likely attempted

01:10:50.850 --> 01:10:56.540
to convince Blackbeard to sail with him, but
Blackbeard sailed off separately, intent on

01:10:56.540 --> 01:11:02.160
staging a few last attacks on vulnerable targets
before his own planned retirement from piracy.

01:11:02.160 --> 01:11:08.400
But only a month later, in November of 1718,
British Lieutenant Robert Maynard caught up

01:11:08.400 --> 01:11:11.600
to Blackbeard and launched a surprise attack.

01:11:11.600 --> 01:11:13.600
Blackbeard quickly fired back.

01:11:13.600 --> 01:11:18.240
His crew wounded or killed 21 of the British
attackers, and boarded the enemy vessel to

01:11:18.240 --> 01:11:19.240
finish the job.

01:11:19.240 --> 01:11:24.370
But a dozen British sailors lay hidden in
the hold— they emerged and charged with

01:11:24.370 --> 01:11:25.370
Maynard.

01:11:25.370 --> 01:11:30.190
Across a deck sleek with blood, in a haze
of musket fire, the giant Blackbeard appeared

01:11:30.190 --> 01:11:35.000
and lunged at the rival captain; Maynard locked
swords with Blackbeard.

01:11:35.000 --> 01:11:39.900
Swinging furiously, the pair dueled until
at last Maynard stuck his challenger.

01:11:39.900 --> 01:11:43.190
The pirate shattered the sword lodged inside
of him.

01:11:43.190 --> 01:11:45.290
Blackbeard reached for his pistol, but Maynard
shot him.

01:11:45.290 --> 01:11:48.450
The other British sailors overwhelmed the
pirate with gunfire and swords.

01:11:48.450 --> 01:11:53.930
“Here was an End of that couragious Brute,
who might have pass’d in the World for a

01:11:53.930 --> 01:11:59.640
Heroe, had he been employ’d in a good Cause...The
Lieutenant caused Black-beard’s Head to

01:11:59.640 --> 01:12:05.690
be severed from his Body, and hung up at the
Bolt-sprit End, then he sailed to Bath-Town,

01:12:05.690 --> 01:12:10.000
to get Relief for his wounded Men.”

01:12:12.172 --> 01:12:16.130
It might have seemed prudent for Charles Vane
to avoid a similar fate.

01:12:16.130 --> 01:12:20.690
He retreated when a French naval ship fired
on him just a day later, but his crew took

01:12:20.690 --> 01:12:23.130
his caution for cowardice.

01:12:23.130 --> 01:12:27.270
In accordance with the pirate code, the crew
exercised their right to vote him out.

01:12:27.270 --> 01:12:30.170
His shipmate Calico Jack Rackham took the
helm.

01:12:30.170 --> 01:12:35.630
With a small gang of loyal supporters, Charles
Vane was sent off in a meager sloop.

01:12:35.630 --> 01:12:40.270
Vane continued to attack and rob merchant
vessels, but soon a dark storm descended upon

01:12:40.270 --> 01:12:41.300
his ship.

01:12:41.300 --> 01:12:44.860
The vessel screeched and teetered over thunderous
swells.

01:12:44.860 --> 01:12:49.000
Vane and the crew were thrashed about the
deck, until waves overtook them.

01:12:49.000 --> 01:12:54.870
The plunder was lost, and the crew was thrown
overboard...

01:12:54.870 --> 01:13:01.620
And yet this wasnt the end.

01:13:01.620 --> 01:13:05.960
Among the drifting wreckage that washed up
on the still shores of a desert island were

01:13:05.960 --> 01:13:11.770
splintered wood, strips of sail, and the captain
himself, Charles Vane.

01:13:11.770 --> 01:13:14.230
The castaway had cheated death.

01:13:14.230 --> 01:13:18.230
He crawled ashore through clumped sand and
cursed his wretched lot.

01:13:18.230 --> 01:13:23.210
Here, on some unknown stretch of land, Vane
languished.

01:13:23.210 --> 01:13:25.500
Days passed under a bleating sun.

01:13:25.500 --> 01:13:35.610
He spent his hours gazing out at a desolate
blue-green horizon, seeing not another soul.

01:13:35.610 --> 01:13:47.150
Vane 
remained brooding on the island for weeks

01:13:47.150 --> 01:13:54.020
until, in early 1719, he spotted his saving
opportunity: an English vessel from Jamaica

01:13:54.020 --> 01:13:55.040
stopped on the island.

01:13:55.040 --> 01:14:00.070
Charles Vane happened to be an old acquaintance
of its commander, Captain Holford.

01:14:00.070 --> 01:14:05.710
a retired buccaneer, Holford knew Vane well
enough to bar the conniving pirate from boarding.

01:14:05.710 --> 01:14:09.830
Still, some part of Holford took pity on the
castaway.

01:14:09.830 --> 01:14:14.870
Rather than arrest Vane outright, Holford
gave his old friend a running start,

01:14:14.870 --> 01:14:20.220
“I am now going down the Bay... and shall
return hither, in about a Month; and if I

01:14:20.220 --> 01:14:25.960
find you upon the Island when I come back,
I’ll carry you to Jamaica, and hang you.”

01:14:25.960 --> 01:14:27.600
Holford sailed off.

01:14:27.600 --> 01:14:30.800
And Vane was left to scour the horizon for
a rescuer.

01:14:30.800 --> 01:14:33.600
Within a few days, another English ship arrived.

01:14:33.600 --> 01:14:37.290
The sailors met the weary castaway and heard
his sympathetic plight.

01:14:37.290 --> 01:14:40.630
Vane gave them a false name and joined their
crew.

01:14:40.630 --> 01:14:42.690
They began to sail out of the bay.

01:14:42.690 --> 01:14:47.070
“One would be apt to think that Vane was
now pretty safe, and likely to escape the

01:14:47.070 --> 01:14:52.290
Fate which his Crimes had merited; but here
a cross Accident happen’d that ruin’d

01:14:52.290 --> 01:14:56.460
all: Holford, returning from the Bay, was
met with by this Ship; the Captains being

01:14:56.460 --> 01:15:00.960
very well acquainted together, Holford was
invited to dine, and as he passed along to

01:15:00.960 --> 01:15:08.130
the Cabin, he chanced to cast his Eye down
the Hold, and there saw Charles Vane at work.”

01:15:08.130 --> 01:15:13.920
Captain Holford arrested Vane at gunpoint,
and carried the chained pirate back to Jamaica.

01:15:13.920 --> 01:15:17.810
There, authorities locked him in a cell.

01:15:17.810 --> 01:15:20.380
The antipiracy efforts of the Crown had worked
well.

01:15:20.380 --> 01:15:24.980
In short order, the last of the great pirates
would disappear, including those who had fled

01:15:24.980 --> 01:15:26.360
eastward.

01:15:26.360 --> 01:15:29.210
Pardoned men like Henry Jennings could retire.

01:15:29.210 --> 01:15:34.580
The thousands who refused the king’s offer
were hunted down and hanged.

01:15:34.580 --> 01:15:38.990
Others lost their lives on the high seas;
some would be dashed against the rocks in

01:15:38.990 --> 01:15:39.990
hurricanes.

01:15:39.990 --> 01:15:42.130
The great Bartholomew Roberts was shot in
the throat.

01:15:42.130 --> 01:15:47.690
And still others, like the brutal Edward Low—who
notoriously forced a man to eat his own broiled

01:15:47.690 --> 01:15:53.918
lips— disappeared.

01:15:53.918 --> 01:16:09.940
Though likely captured and killed, a wishful Caribbean criminal might imagine missing pirates like Low and Henry Every had made it out alive— that they had cheated the hangman and lived out the rest of their days sipping rum amidst their plunder.

01:16:09.940 --> 01:16:15.940
As for Charles Vane, a year in prison culminated
in a stuffy trial, flanked by crimson-clad

01:16:15.940 --> 01:16:17.350
colonial guardsmen.

01:16:17.350 --> 01:16:24.210
YOn March 29th in the year 1721, Vane was
walked to Gallows Point in the once lawless

01:16:24.210 --> 01:16:30.360
Port Royal, and hanged by the neck until he
died.

01:16:30.360 --> 01:16:34.900
His executioners placed his lifeless body
in a cage, which teetered for years at the

01:16:34.900 --> 01:16:39.770
harbor’s entrance of Gun Cay, such that
the rotting flesh of Charles Vane nourished

01:16:39.770 --> 01:16:45.940
the passing crow, and his slumping visage
prophesied the fate of all who sailed under

01:16:45.940 --> 01:16:48.720
the black flag.

01:17:00.975 --> 01:17:03.060
To Marquis de Lafayette,
“...Let me ask you my Dr. Marquis, in such

01:17:03.060 --> 01:17:08.540
an enlightened, in such a liberal age, how
is it possible the great maritime powers of

01:17:08.540 --> 01:17:15.210
Europe should submit to pay an annual tribute
to the little piratical States of Barbary?

01:17:15.210 --> 01:17:20.860
Would to Heaven we had a navy able to reform
those enemies to mankind, or crush them into

01:17:20.860 --> 01:17:23.790
non-existence.”

01:17:23.790 --> 01:17:28.771
The Barbary States which had so terrified
Europe under the likes of Dragut, had— over

01:17:28.771 --> 01:17:34.420
the centuries— modified the primary means
by which they extracted wealth from the seas.

01:17:34.420 --> 01:17:40.060
They now guaranteed safe passage for the feeble
merchant ships that crossed the Mediterranean,

01:17:40.060 --> 01:17:45.790
so long as their mother countries submitted
to pay an exorbitant tribute to the Barbary

01:17:45.790 --> 01:17:46.790
corsair leaders.

01:17:46.790 --> 01:17:51.900
If not, the Barbary corsairs would do what
they had always done; capture the boats and

01:17:51.900 --> 01:17:56.790
kill, ransom, or enslave everyone aboard.

01:17:56.790 --> 01:18:02.450
The corsairs even forced the fledgling United
States of America to pay the tribute for passage

01:18:02.450 --> 01:18:03.730
into the Mediterranean.

01:18:03.730 --> 01:18:09.830
By 1797, the Americans had paid, in total,
a fifth of their annual budget to the Barbary

01:18:09.830 --> 01:18:11.170
states.

01:18:11.170 --> 01:18:15.950
Despite the protests of taxpayers, the United
States had little choice but to pay— they

01:18:15.950 --> 01:18:19.880
lacked the naval might to counter the growing
Barbary demands.

01:18:19.880 --> 01:18:24.980
Their eventual refusal to pay prompted the
corsairs to capture American vessels and sell

01:18:24.980 --> 01:18:29.860
the men aboard in their slave markets; the
monetary loss, buttressed by the image of

01:18:29.860 --> 01:18:33.730
enslaved white Americans, proved galvanizing.

01:18:33.730 --> 01:18:39.410
President Thomas Jefferson raised an expensive
fleet that sailed out to bombard the Barbary

01:18:39.410 --> 01:18:46.780
States.

01:18:46.780 --> 01:18:51.860
In the coming years, warships would burn and
whole crews would perish in the fighting.

01:18:51.860 --> 01:18:57.110
Sweden and Sicily also took up arms against
their Barbary enemies until, at last, the

01:18:57.110 --> 01:19:03.780
Barbary States relented. 1815 saw a three-day
Second Barbary War and a renewed European

01:19:03.780 --> 01:19:08.500
commitment to suppress the corsairs after
the Napoleonic Wars.

01:19:08.500 --> 01:19:14.380
This was the downfall of these once powerful
pirates, who had roamed the seas since the

01:19:14.380 --> 01:19:20.290
Middle Ages.

01:19:20.290 --> 01:19:25.400
By that time, the War of 1812 had ended, and
many privateers employed by the British and

01:19:25.400 --> 01:19:27.390
Americans found themselves without work.

01:19:27.390 --> 01:19:32.440
A simultaneous wave of revolution severed
Spain’s hold on her New World colonies.

01:19:32.440 --> 01:19:39.000
The coasts of Latin America and the Caribbean
thus became, once again, fertile ground for

01:19:39.000 --> 01:19:40.000
pirates.

01:19:40.000 --> 01:19:45.360
But the combined efforts of industrializing
world powers undermined their strength.

01:19:45.360 --> 01:19:49.290
President James Monroe spearheaded the efforts
to eradicate pirates.

01:19:49.290 --> 01:19:54.360
Meanwhile, state-sanctioned forms of armed
robbery also disappeared from the high seas,

01:19:54.360 --> 01:19:58.060
as nations moved away from the practice of
privateering.

01:19:58.060 --> 01:20:04.230
These trends were codified in anti-privateering
proclamations and multinational movements,

01:20:04.230 --> 01:20:10.350
such that the seas once harassed by the Sea
Peoples, the Victual Brothers, and the buccaneers,

01:20:10.350 --> 01:20:15.920
now waxed and waned a little more quietly,
and trade ships followed their routes with

01:20:15.920 --> 01:20:16.920
a little more monotony.

01:20:16.920 --> 01:20:23.160
A man called Charles Gibbs graced these closing
pages of pirate history without an ounce of

01:20:23.160 --> 01:20:24.210
shame.

01:20:24.210 --> 01:20:29.650
When arrested and convincted in 1831, the
American pirate admitted to the murder of

01:20:29.650 --> 01:20:36.020
400 people and readily told of buried treasure,
“The quantity of money buried in different

01:20:36.020 --> 01:20:39.710
parts of the West India Islands is beyond
calculation.

01:20:39.710 --> 01:20:45.770
Immense treasures have been secreted there;
a great portion of which will never be found.”

01:20:45.770 --> 01:20:51.920
Gibbs was brought to the very same speck of
land that would usher in a new global, urbanized

01:20:51.920 --> 01:20:52.930
age.

01:20:52.930 --> 01:20:58.420
Under the gaze of Lady Liberty, Ellis Island
would soon receive millions of hopeful immigrants

01:20:58.420 --> 01:21:00.330
into the United States.

01:21:00.330 --> 01:21:09.710
It was there that Charles Gibbs was hanged.

01:21:09.710 --> 01:21:16.670
Piracy had always been complicated; theft,
torture, murder, and enslavement were perpetrated

01:21:16.670 --> 01:21:23.580
on the high seas by peoples, states and individuals
possessing varying degrees of legal support.

01:21:23.580 --> 01:21:27.670
Men like Francis Drake or Henry Morgan might
have recoiled at the assertion that they were

01:21:27.670 --> 01:21:35.100
anything but law-abiding patriots; they fancied
themselves heroes to be lauded, and not outlaws

01:21:35.100 --> 01:21:36.610
to be feared.

01:21:36.610 --> 01:21:39.950
Charles Gibbs had seemed different.

01:21:39.950 --> 01:21:44.080
From the alleged buried treasure to the absurd
number of deaths for which he took credit,

01:21:44.080 --> 01:21:49.850
it is thought that much of his post-conviction
confession was fabricated—that perhaps the

01:21:49.850 --> 01:21:56.030
ill-fated Gibbs desired, in his final days,
to hoist his legend alongside the pantheon

01:21:56.030 --> 01:22:03.320
of pirates who once roamed these waters: outlaws
like Blackbeard, Henry Avery, and Charles

01:22:03.320 --> 01:22:08.380
Vane— men for whom piracy was not merely
maritime banditry borne out of desperation,

01:22:08.380 --> 01:22:15.690
but a proud culture, a defiant flag raised
brutally against imperial excess— an unjust

01:22:15.690 --> 01:22:22.780
enterprise unburdened by the pretense of altruism,
where shameless greed and sadism might lie

01:22:22.780 --> 01:22:29.330
alongside nascent republicanism in garish
harmony.

01:22:29.330 --> 01:22:35.580
It was at the tail end of the Golden Age,
in July of 1723, when a periodical known as

01:22:35.580 --> 01:22:40.550
the Monthly Mercury recounted a bit of news
from the West Indies.

01:22:40.550 --> 01:22:47.750
“This Day, 26 of the Pirates taken by his
Majesty Ship the Greyhound, Captain Solgard,

01:22:47.750 --> 01:22:53.170
were executed here...Their black Flag, under
which they had committed abundance of Pyracies

01:22:53.170 --> 01:22:57.610
and Murders, was affix'd to one Corner of
the Gallows.

01:22:57.610 --> 01:23:03.310
It had in it the Portraiture of Death, with
an Hour-Glass in one Hand, and a Dart in the

01:23:03.310 --> 01:23:09.000
other, striking into a Heart, and three Drops
of Blood delineated as falling from it.

01:23:09.000 --> 01:23:16.560
This Flag they called Old Roger, and us'd
to say, They would live and die under it.”

